04.codex.stream\nX04.md\nX05.claude.json\nX05.codex-prompt.txt\nX05.codex.json\nX05.codex.stream\nX05.md\nX06.claude.json\nX06.codex-prompt.txt\nX06.codex.json\nX06.codex.stream\nX06.md\nX07.claude.json\nX07.codex-prompt.txt\nX07.codex.json\nX07.codex.stream\nX07.md\nX08.claude.json\nX08.codex-prompt.txt\nX08.codex.json\nX08.codex.stream\nX08.md\ninterrupted\n\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/reader-packets/interrupted:\nX04.codex.json.empty\nX04.codex.stream\nX05.codex.json.empty\nX05.codex.stream\nX08.codex.json.empty\nX08.codex.stream\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in BLIND{3,4,5,6}-RESULT.md judging-v13s.txt; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in BLIND{3,4,5,6}-RESULT.md judging-v13s.txt; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"BLIND3-RESULT.md\n     1\t# judging-v13s blind test, third run after D742: result (SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D742])\n     2\t\n     3\t**STOP on one registered line, as written** (council round 57 confirms): \"an accepted reply with a named defect (marked by both readers …)\".\n     4\t- **R01 (S, Claude writer).** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on F14: the outcome is a range of $679.25–745B, and rung 4\n     5\t  ($700–800B) does not hold all of it.\n     6\t- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n     7\t  with the reason in reasons\". The answer gives that reason: \"the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4\". The code (S6) checks\n     8\t  overlap.\n     9\t- **The cause is my reader brief.** Its definition of broken structure reads \"a rung whose band does not hold the outcome\", without the\n    10\t  schema's range clause. Both readers applied the brief as written. No earlier run had a range that crossed a rung edge, so the gap was\n    11\t  not exercised before.\n    12\t\n    13\tEverything else in the run is clean:\n    14\t- **Writers:** 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, so there was no refusal review.\n    15\t- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n    16\t  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n    17\t- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n    18\t- **The D742 rule was followed by every P-family writer.** All six recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark, then\n    19\t  computed Q4 from it. None computed a guidance rate straight into the calculation.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    22\t`schema.json`. The tally is in `TALLY.json`.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    25\t\n    26\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| P (joint) | v4 416.3, v8 386.1, both rung 2 | v4 420.9, v8 391.0, both rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v4 | 419.3, rung 2 | 416.3, rung 2 |\n    30\t| P-v8 | 387.4, rung 2 | 388.1, rung 2 |\n    31\t| S (CY2026) | 679.25–745, rung 4 | 670.5, rung 3 |\n    32\t| O | 9–20, rung 1 | 16, rung 1 |\n    33\t| E | 37, rung 3 | 34, rung 3 |\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    36\t\n    37\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    38\t- R01 broken structure (both readers' marks), explained above;\n    39\t- R01 \"two forecasts\" (Microsoft backed out of the four-company tallies, a reader matter through `rests_on`);\n    40\t- two \"plus or minus 2%\" midpoint marks (R02, R09) and a rounding difference within tolerance (R08). In all, both adjudicators\n    41\t  rejected 8 marks covering 7 distinct allegations (F14 was marked twice);\n    42\t- R06's \"number misread\" claims on the $18B schedule and the Lancium tranche.\n    43\t\n    44\t**Support:**\n    45\t- **R07 (S, Codex writer) is settled \"not supported\".** Its rung rests on a 90% data-center share, while the passages put the share near\n    46\t  100%:\n    47\t  - e1947: more than $730B \"on AI infrastructure\" against $720–745B of capex;\n    48\t  - e2759 and e5376: NVIDIA's \"nearly $800 billion\" for the top five, written both as capex and as data-center capex;\n    49\t  - Meta's own figures: about 96%.\n    50\t  At 94% or more, the same $745B gives rung 4. The reply is accepted and publishable, but its rung does not follow from the evidence.\n    51\t  This is not a registered stop line (usable is counted per case, and S has R01). It is a real judgement error in an answer that would\n    52\t  publish.\n    53\t- **R06 (O, Claude writer) is split again** on the $18B reading: Codex says not supported, Claude says supported. This is the same\n    54\t  passage as in both earlier runs.\n    55\t- Every other forecast is supported by both readers or both adjudicators.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. What the test shows\n    58\t\n    59\t- **The writer-side contract holds.** In three runs, the request has moved from 11 of 12 accepted with a checker error (run 1), to 11 of\n    60\t  12 with a request contradiction (run 2), to 12 of 12 with no refusal (run 3). D742's rule was followed by all six writers it applies\n    61\t  to.\n    62\t- **The stop this time is in the measuring instrument.** The reader brief's definition is narrower than the format the writer was\n    63\t  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n    64\t- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n    65\t  kind of error the paid re-send is meant to measure in gpt-6-luna.\n    66\t- **Council round 57 found two more points** (`codex-r57.md`):\n    67\t  - R01, the only usable S reply, marks Zacks's $720–745B of *total* capex as the market reference for *data-center* capex. Claude's\n    68\t    reader noticed it in its ground but filed no named defect.\n    69\t  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n    70\t    sits in rung 2.\n    71\t- **Open disclosures:**\n    72\t  - O's $18B reading (split three runs running);\n    73\t  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n    74\t  - R07's unsupported share.\n    75\t\n    76\t## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n    77\t\n    78\tFresh readers re-read the same 12 replies under the corrected range rule. Two controls were mixed in, under a fresh shuffle Q01–Q14.\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The range repair works.**\n    81\t  - C-disjoint was marked broken structure by both readers.\n    82\t  - C-overlap was marked by neither.\n    83\t  - R01 (now Q07), the range across a rung edge, was not marked broken structure by either reader.\n    84\t- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\n    85\t  - Both readers marked \"number misread\" on F6: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as its midpoint, 108.\n    86\t  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n    87\t    or its midpoint as value\".\n    88\t  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n    89\t- **The class.** The readers' packet carries the user text and the answer, but not the format rules the writer was given. The\n    90\t  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n    91\t  (the range, the midpoint) come from this one gap.\n    92\t- **C-overlap's other mark is my error.** Both readers marked \"two forecasts\" on C-overlap because my control kept E's original text\n    93\t  (\"85% … $37 billion\") beside the new range. The readers were right to flag the inconsistency. It does not touch the range rule the\n    94\t  control tests.\n    95\t- **Judgement noise:**\n    96\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n    97\t    readers now.\n    98\t  - S's Claude reply (Q07) is split at both levels this time: Codex says not supported and upholds two marks; Claude rejects them.\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   101\t\n   102\t## 5. The second blinded re-read (D744, Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; `reread2-packets/`, `reread2-adjudication/`, `REREAD2-TALLY.json`)\n   103\t\n   104\tReaders now get the same rule text as adjudicators (`blind_rules.py`). There were five clean controls, under a fresh shuffle T01–T17.\n   105\t\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\nBLIND4-RESULT.md\n     1\t# The D745 targeted test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745] and scored by\n     4\t`blind4.py tally` (`TALLY.json`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL (registered line \"every S and E reply has a settled yes\")\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Case | Writer | Shape | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers, one per family,\n    44\t    said not supported, so no adjudication was needed (corrected after council round 61, which caught \"all four readers\").\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 4. What the paid re-send would meet\n    65\t\n    66\tUnder the paid gate's lines (`PAID-GATE.md` §4), a published singleton with a \"deciding gap\" stops the run. A deciding gap includes \"an\n    67\tunsized adjustment decides its band\". Where the two readers disagree, the third reader is Codex. On blind4's evidence, a published E forecast built like\n    68\tthese replies risks being marked by Codex, and the run would stop.\n    69\t- **Two corrections from council round 61, both checked:**\n    70\t  - An unresolved singleton is published, shown as unresolved (`calc_small.publish_decision`: \"held\" only when a declared relation\n    71\t    joins it to an answered member). The paid verdict inspects published singleton cards for defects (`paid_gate.py`, verdict), so it\n    72\t    is not exempt.\n    73\t  - The paid S singleton asks about CY2027 (`paid_gate.py` SINGLETONS: old-2 v6 8595). This test's S asks about CY2026 (`SMALL-DESIGN.md`\n    74\t    §5: old-3 v6 9043).\n    75\t- These votes are about support. They show a risk to the paid run, not a measured rate at which it would stop.\n    76\t\n    77\t## 5. Not done\n    78\t\n    79\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. No request text changed after the registration.\n    80\t\n    81\t## 6. The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (D747): FAIL on a stop line\n    82\t\n    83\tRegistered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D747], reviewed by council round 62 (`codex-r62.md`), and scored by `blind4.py reread-tally`\n    84\t(`REREAD-TALLY.json`, `reread-reasons.json`). Packets W01–W09 are in `reread-packets/`, adjudications in `reread-adjudication/`, and\n    85\tthe key is `reread-key.json`.\n    86\t\n    87\t| Packet | Answer | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Registered line |\n    88\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    89\t| W01 | U01 vague unresolved control | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    90\t| W04 | U05 E Codex, unsized 10% cut | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    91\t| W09 | U03 E, unsized 15% cut | no, no | (none) | no | expects no: ok |\n    92\t| W02 | U06 E Claude, 1.8x × 90% Vera share | no, no | (none) | no | must settle: ok |\n    93\t| W05 | U04 S justified unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | must settle: ok |\n    94\t| W08 | U08 S Codex, $25B deduction | yes, yes | (none) | yes | must settle: ok |\n    95\t| W07 | U02 S Claude, 98% share | no, yes | no, yes | split | expects no: MISS |\n    96\t| W06 | T15 E Claude, 85% growth bracket | yes, no | yes, no | split | expects yes: MISS |\n    97\t| W03 | T10 S Claude, 95–100% share | no, yes | no, yes | split | must settle: MISS |\n    98\t\n    99\t- **The stop.** On W07 (U02), both adjudicators upheld Codex's \"part above or added into its whole\" (F12, F11, F15). The answer\n   100\t  calls F12's \"more than $730 billion\" of AI infrastructure \"the supported floor\" for data-center spending, while its own total\n   101\t  spending (F11) is $720B and its data-center outcome (F15) $705.6B. The code did not catch it, because F12 is a source figure the\n   102\t  answer uses as a floor only in its prose.\n   103\t- **The reasons behind the six settled answers were read by Claude and recorded as compatible** (`reread-reasons.json`). Each pair\n   104\t  applies the same reading. On W08, for example, both say the same four companies' AI-infrastructure figure and Meta's AI share explain\n   105\t  why a deduction under $45B is more likely, and both note that this does not measure the deduction directly.\n   106\t- **What the ruling changed.** Of the four answers that split in the targeted test, three now settle in both families, with\n   107\t  compatible reasons: U04 yes, U06 no, U08 yes. All four controls and fixed cuts read as expected.\n   108\t- **The remaining splits no longer follow model family.**\n   109\t  - Codex is the stricter reader on S (T10, U02). Claude is the stricter reader on E (T15).\n   110\t  - T15 was accepted by both families in the second re-read and now splits.\n   111\t  - Read with the third-run S Codex reply's earlier no → yes → no, this shows single readers varying on boundary answers.\n   112\t- **A process error, shared by Claude and the council.** The registration required five boundary answers to settle, or to settle as\n   113\t  expected, but nobody estimated how often single readers split on boundary answers. AGENTS §7.1 says \"a conjunction over many items\n   114\t  fails by chance\". That clause of the pass line could fail on reader variation alone. The stop on U02 does not depend on it.\nBLIND5-RESULT.md\n     1\t# The D749 writer test of the E writer repair: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D749], with council round 64's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind5.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `ac707fa9…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: INCONCLUSIVE (two support splits, X05 and X08); no line failed\n     7\t\n     8\t**A correction, made after council round 65 (`codex-r65.md`).** I first recorded X08's two \"yes\" readings as compatible, although\n     9\tmy own note named the tension. The Claude reader read e1947 as a floor (\"at least $730B\") while accepting $710.4B. The Codex reader\n    10\tread it as a guide. Under the registered procedure, matching votes with incompatible reasons go to adjudication.\n    11\t- X08 was sent to adjudication. Both adjudicators said yes. The Claude adjudicator again read a floor above the outcome, and the\n    12\t  Codex adjudicator did not reconcile the $730B figure.\n    13\t- X08 is recorded as split (`reasons.json`). The row below is corrected, and the verdict stays INCONCLUSIVE.\n    14\t\n    15\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n    16\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    17\t| X06 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    18\t| X05 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($42B: 2.1x, 100% Vera) | no, yes | no, yes | split | inconclusive |\n    19\t| X02 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($730B estimate from the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    20\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($740B × 96% = $710.4B) | yes, yes (reasons incompatible: floor against guide) | yes, yes (reasons incompatible again) | split | inconclusive |\n    21\t| X01 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($383.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    22\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($380.8B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    23\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut (blind4 U05) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    24\t| X07 | control: impossible floor (blind4 U02) | forecast | floor marked by Codex only | floor upheld by both | floor settled | ok |\n    25\t\n    26\t- **The code** accepted all six fresh replies, and all six are publishable.\n    27\t- **No defect** was marked on a fresh reply.\n    28\t- **The reasons** behind the five settled fresh pairs and the two controls were read by Claude and recorded as compatible\n    29\t  (`reasons.json`). X08 is corrected to incompatible, as above.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. What changed against the targeted test (D745), same cases\n    32\t\n    33\t- **E.** The Codex writer no longer invents a cut. It answers unresolved and names the gap: both the FY2027 base and the FY2028\n    34\t  increase are projections, and no passage gives a shipment run-rate, an order with units and prices, or a deployment schedule. Both\n    35\t  readers accept it. In the targeted test, the same writer type cut the reference by 10% and was settled not supported.\n    36\t- **S.** Both fresh forecasts are supported by both families. In the targeted test both split.\n    37\t- **P-v8.** Both fresh replies are supported forecasts, as before. The repair did not cause withholding on the supported case.\n    38\t- **The controls.** Both read as expected.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 3. The one split, read against its passages (Claude's own trace reading)\n    41\t\n    42\t- **The answer.** E's Claude writer takes management's \"more than double\" in full, at 2.1x on $20B with Vera as 100%, giving $42B,\n    43\t  rung 2. It argues the FY2027 base \"is reaffirmed for the current year, so the CPU line is already selling at volume rather than a\n    44\t  product with no shipments\".\n    45\t- **The passages it saw (15).** None shows Vera shipments, reported Vera revenue or orders:\n    46\t  - e4689: \"will be deployed\";\n    47\t  - e5044: Anthropic \"is evaluating\";\n    48\t  - e4856: the $20B is a \"potential\";\n    49\t  - e3660: management \"still expects\" $20B, which is an outlook;\n    50\t  - the AMD benchmarks (e5399–e5404) show only that a Vera platform exists to be tested.\n    51\t- **The request's own rule** (bullet 2) says a target for a product with no shipments yet \"is taken in full only when the evidence\n    52\t  shows the volume in hand\".\n    53\t- **On the passages, the premise that decides this answer is not shown.**\n    54\t  - The Codex reader and the Codex adjudicator say so.\n    55\t  - The Claude adjudicator accepted the reaffirmed outlook and the benchmarked platform as enough.\n    56\t  - This is a question of fact against an existing rule, not a gap in Alex's D747 words. Where the families differ is whether a\n    57\t    reaffirmed outlook counts as \"volume in hand\".\n    58\t\n    59\t## 4. A trace note the readers did not mark (it became the X08 split after the correction)\n    60\t\n    61\t- **What the answer says.** S's Claude reply (X08) says data-center capex \"contains all AI capex plus non-AI cloud\n    62\t  capacity\". With e1947's more than $730B of AI infrastructure, that implies data-center capex above $730B, while its own outcome is\n    63\t  $710.4B.\n    64\t- **What it means.** The tension runs in the conservative direction and does not move the band (both figures are in rung 4).\n    65\t  Neither reader marked it. It is disclosed, not scored.\n    66\t\n    67\t## 5. Not done\n    68\t\n    69\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\nBLIND6-RESULT.md\n     1\t# The D750 second writer test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D750], with council round 66's\n     4\tchanges, and scored by `blind6.py tally` (`TALLY.json`, `reasons.json`). The request is `judging-v13s.txt`, sha256 `54f5847d…`.\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL on one settled line\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Answer | Shape, rung | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| X06 | fresh E Claude | forecast, rung 3 ($35B: 1.75x pace) | yes, no | no, no | **no** | **FAIL** |\n    11\t| X07 | fresh E Codex | unresolved | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    12\t| X05 | fresh S Codex | forecast, rung 4 ($692.9–737.6B, central about $715B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    13\t| X08 | fresh S Claude | forecast, rung 4 ($708.1B: $715–745B × 97%) | yes, yes; Codex marked two defects | defects: uphold, reject | yes; two defect splits | inconclusive |\n    14\t| X02 | fresh P-v8 Claude | forecast, rung 2 ($385.9B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    15\t| X04 | fresh P-v8 Codex | forecast, rung 2 ($377.7B) | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| X03 | control: invented 10% cut | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    17\t| X01 | control: impossible floor | forecast | floor marked by both | (support only) | floor settled | ok |\n    18\t\n    19\t- **The failure.** X06's writer took the market pace (about 1.48–1.5x) as a floor and management's \"more than double\" as a\n    20\t  ceiling, and chose 1.75x, the midpoint.\n    21\t  - Both adjudicators: the passages state \"more than double\" as a minimum; the midpoint has no source; the reasons for a miss\n    22\t    (one customer evaluating, a competitor's own benchmarks, missing volume figures) size or bound no shortfall below $40B. The\n    23\t    answer shows only that the outcome might fall short.\n    24\t- **The defect splits on X08.**\n    25\t  - F15 = 130 ÷ (125–145) gives a range whose top end is 104%. The Codex adjudicator calls that a part above its whole. The Claude\n    26\t    adjudicator calls it the mechanical end of a range ratio that the format requires.\n    27\t  - F5, the outside total-capex range, is marked as the market's reference for data-center spending. The Codex adjudicator says this\n    28\t    implies a 100% share. The Claude adjudicator says F5 is a quoted outside figure, as the reference rule allows, and that the\n    29\t    deduction is set out separately.\n    30\t- **The reasons and premises** behind every settled S and E vote were read and recorded (`reasons.json`). X05 and X08's readers read\n    31\t  the same four companies' AI-infrastructure plan as a plan that can slip, not as a floor, which fits each answer's own outcome.\n    32\t- **A run incident, disclosed.** The background command running the eight Codex readers was terminated (exit 144) while three\n    33\t  readers were still running. X04, X05 and X08 had empty outputs, kept in `reader-packets/interrupted/`. Fresh Codex readers were\n    34\t  run for those three. No reading was discarded after it was seen.\n    35\t\n    36\t## 2. The E record across the four runs (same case, same passages)\n    37\t\n    38\t| Run | Codex writer | Claude writer |\n    39\t|---|---|---|\n    40\t| third run (D742 request) | 85% realisation, $34B; settled no | 1.85x bracket, $37B; yes in re-read 2, split under D747 |\n    41\t| targeted test (D745) | 90% realisation, $36B; settled no | 1.8x × 90% share, $32.4B; split, then no under D747 |\n    42\t| D749 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 2.1x in full, $42B, asserting \"already selling at volume\"; split |\n    43\t| D750 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 1.75x bracket midpoint, $35B; settled no |\n    44\t\n    45\t- **The Codex writer.** The repair (D749, D750) moved it from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, twice.\n    46\t- **The Claude writer.** It forecasts every time, by a different route each time, and has never been settled supported under the\n    47\t  current definitions.\n    48\t- **S** converged over D749 and D750: all four fresh forecasts are supported. Only defect marks on one answer remain split.\n    49\t- **P-v8** has been supported throughout.\n    50\t\n    51\t## 3. Not done\n    52\t\n    53\tNothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\njudging-v13s.txt\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
