{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba PAID-GATE.md | sed -n '1,42p;252,285p' && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '32,58p;490,550p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba PAID-GATE.md | sed -n '1,42p;252,285p' && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '32,58p;490,550p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# judging-v13 paid gate: plan, harness and reading rule (registered before any send; D725, D729, D730)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Result (D733): STOP.** All 72 answers were refused, and $0.3677 was spent; see `PAID-GATE-RESULT.md`.\n     4\t\n     5\t## 1. What is sent\n     6\t\n     7\t`paid_gate.py plan paid-plan.json` builds 12 requests from the stored runs.\n     8\t\n     9\t| Unit | Arm | Members | Evidence shown |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| pair-old-1 (09-20: v4 total, v8 Data Center) | joint; separate-v4; separate-v8 | both; one; one | the union of both stored requests' evidence in view (237 items), in every arm |\n    12\t| pair-old-2 (v14, v9) | joint; separate-v14; separate-v9 | as above | 237 |\n    13\t| pair-old-3 (v3, v35) | joint; separate-v3; separate-v35 | as above | 260 |\n    14\t| single-O (old-2 v29, equity commitments) | single | v29 | its own request's (35) |\n    15\t| single-S (old-2 v6, four hyperscalers' 2027 capex) | single | v6 | 31 |\n    16\t| single-E (old-3 v41, FY2028 Vera CPU) | single | v41 | 15 |\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Each request:** judging-v13's system text (`judging-v13-draft.txt`, 4,373 characters); the family header with the exact outcome\n    19\t  keys; the evidence; the themes once. No earlier forecast is shown (D728). The strict v13 schema.\n    20\t- **The model:** poc-v118's re-judging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium effort, 16,000 output tokens).\n    21\t- **Draws:** three per request, so 36 first attempts.\n    22\t- **Retry:** a calculation the checks refuse, or a schema-invalid answer, is retried once with the reasons. A second refusal makes the\n    23\t  unit unresolved.\n    24\t- **What is scored:** the first attempt, on both arms (§3f). Retries are sent and recorded for diagnosis only.\n    25\t- **The separate arm:** its two first answers per pair and draw are merged and checked over the pair's family (C3, C4 across the two).\n    26\t\n    27\t## 2. Spend and stops\n    28\t\n    29\t- **The ledger:** one, at Alex's ceiling (`paid_gate.py send <folder> <ceiling> paid-plan.json` refuses any other ceiling).\n    30\t- **Budget:** the real budget transport and stop gate, which reserve each request's worst case before sending. After a stop, nothing\n    31\t  more is sent, and the remaining units are recorded as not sent.\n    32\t- **Estimate** (at `hivemind.model.PRICES`: gpt-6-luna, $0.10 input and $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as\n    33\t  characters / 4):\n    34\t  - a pair request is about 34–38k input tokens and a singleton 6–9k;\n    35\t  - the 36 first attempts cost about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n    36\t  - the transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request.\n    37\t  - **Proposed ceiling: $0.50.** It covers every scored attempt. Retries come last (§3g), so a ceiling stop among them leaves the\n    38\t    scored test complete.\n    39\t- **Sends:** one at a time, every first attempt before any retry (§3g). Each send has its own archive, so no draw is answered from\n    40\t  another.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. The $0 rehearsal (`paid_rehearsal.py`, `rehearsal.log`: 16 of 16)\n   252\t## 4. The reading rule (fixed now, before any answer exists)\n   253\t\n   254\t- **Packets:** 27 (`paid_gate.py packets`): 9 joint pair-draws, 9 reconciled separate pair-draws and 9 singleton draws. The labels are\n   255\t  shuffled, the arm is hidden, and the key is kept in `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/`.\n   256\t  - Each packet shows, per card (K1, K2), the question, the ladder, every passage the final answer names (as the request showed it),\n   257\t    its figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. Nothing about attempts or the code's decision is\n   258\t    shown (§3e).\n   259\t  - A packet the code did not publish (held, unresolved, not reconciled) is read too. The verdict, not the reader, applies the code's\n   260\t    decision: its cards cannot count as usable.\n   261\t  - The readers receive `packets.md` exactly, with its key-commitment line. The readings file carries that text's sha256.\n   262\t- **Readers:** two fresh readers of different families, a Codex session and a Claude subagent, each blind to the other and to the arm.\n   263\t  For each card each answers four questions, yes or no, with the passage that decides:\n   264\t  1. **usable:** the outcome is usable and supported by the shown passages it rests on;\n   265\t  2. **contradiction:** with the other card of its packet, it states a hard numerical contradiction;\n   266\t  3. **internal_reference:** the system's own forecast is presented as the market's expectation;\n   267\t  4. **deciding_gap:** an unresolved overlap, a change of scope or an unsized adjustment decides its band.\n   268\t- **Agreement:** where the two readers agree, that is the reading. Where they disagree, a third fresh reader (Codex) reads the card with\n   269\t  both readings and the passages, and its answer decides. No reading is changed after the verdict is computed.\n   270\t- **Verdict** (`paid_gate.py verdict`, D725's lines, on the first attempts; computed once and sealed, §3f):\n   271\t  - **PASS:** the joint arm has both cards usable in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot, and at least 3 more usable\n   272\t    pairs than the separate arm; and zero published defects (contradiction, internal reference or deciding gap) across the joint and\n   273\t    singleton answers.\n   274\t  - **STOP:** any such published defect; a coverage line missed; a gain under 3.\n   275\t  - **INCOMPLETE:** a packet or a card missing a reading, or a unit not sent.\n   276\t  - This is a small design test, not release acceptance.\n   277\t\n   278\t## 5. What the result decides\n   279\t\n   280\t- **PASS:** judging-v13 earns a production design: automatic families in matching, lifecycle and the request builder, each a further\n   281\t  step Alex opens.\n   282\t- **STOP or INCOMPLETE:** the line goes back to Alex with the readings, and nothing is tuned on these 36 answers.\n    32\t## 2. The rules code enforces (revised after round 52, `codex-r52.md`; the revision is marked [52])\n    33\t\n    34\t**[52] Shared definitions.**\n    35\t- **Uses.** A figure *uses* another when the other is among its `inputs`, at any depth. An estimate's `rests_on` is a judgement, not\n    36\t  a use. Code records it for readers, and an estimate is the value the figures above it use.\n    37\t- **Recomputed values.** The published value of a computed figure is the recomputed one, a point or a range. S3, S4 and S6 compare\n    38\t  recomputed values. S2's tolerance applies only between a declared value and its recomputed value.\n    39\t\n    40\t| # | Defect | Rule [52] (as the request states it) | Where stated |\n    41\t|---|---|---|---|\n    42\t| S1 | a number misread | A source figure names its passage (`evidence_id`) and copies the number exactly as the quote writes it (`quote_number`). The number is in US dollars ($, US$) with its scale (trillion, billion, bn, million), or is a percentage, or is one of the words double, triple or twice. The value equals it, with money in billions. A minus written before it makes the value negative. A qualifier written just before it (or \"or more\" / \"or less\" just after) goes in `qualifier`. A written range (\"$3–4 billion\", \"between $3 billion and $4 billion\") gives low and high. \"$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%\" is copied whole, and gives its range as low and high or its midpoint as value. Money in another currency cannot be a source. | `kind`, `evidence_id`, `quote_number`, `qualifier`, `unit` |\n    43\t| S2 | wrong arithmetic | A computed figure equals its operation's result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). Units are those `operation` states for each operation; a sum or difference adds one unit. A range input gives a range: both extremes are computed first, and a midpoint may be declared as value. Division by a range that contains zero is refused. | `operation` |\n    44\t| S3 | a part above its whole | For each period the Relations line lists, and for the outcomes, the part's lowest recomputed value used for that period may not exceed the whole's highest. | the family header |\n    45\t| S8 | a part added into its whole; periods misadded | A sum never adds a figure that uses (or is) the part's quantity to one that uses (or is) the whole's: the whole already includes the part. A difference may subtract the part from the whole only to give the rest, another scope. When a sum's or difference's inputs are periods of one quantity, each period is written FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and together they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4). An estimate that rests on both the part and the whole is recorded for readers. | `operation`; `what.period`; the family header |\n    46\t| S4 | two forecasts of one quarter | Every number the outcomes use for one quantity and period is the same figure. Compute each quantity and period once, as a point or a range, and use that figure wherever it is needed. (Guidance or consensus beneath that figure is fine; a second figure of the same quantity and period reached on another path is not.) | the family header; `figures` |\n    47\t| S5 | an own estimate passed off as the market's | The figure marked in `reference_for` and every figure beneath it (inputs and rests_on) is a source, or computed with no basis forecast. None is an estimate. At least one is a quoted consensus, guidance or third_party figure, which may be the marked figure itself. A forecast member has exactly one marked reference figure or a `reference_unavailable` reason. | `reference_for`; `reference_unavailable` |\n    48\t| S6 | structure: the answer's number | A forecast member has exactly one figure marked in `outcome_for`. It is a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast (the analyst's own forecast, even when it agrees with consensus). Its `what` is the member's outcome key, its unit is USD bn, and its rung is 1 to the ladder's length. The rung's band (lower edge included, upper excluded) holds the recomputed outcome, or overlaps it for a range. | `outcome_for`; `rung` |\n    49\t| S7 | structure: the calculation | Ids are unique and never equal a passage id. `inputs` name figures; `rests_on` names figures or passages. No figure uses itself. A figure has value, or low and high (low ≤ high), never both. A source has `evidence_id` and `quote_number`; a computed figure has `inputs` and `operation`; an estimate has `basis_text`. Basis forecast is never on a source. There is one answer per member. An unresolved answer has `why`, rung null, `reference_unavailable` null and no marked figure. | the field descriptions |\n    50\t\n    51\t**[52] Operation units (stated in `operation`):**\n    52\t- sum and difference: one unit in, the same out.\n    53\t- product: USD bn × x → USD bn; x × x → x; % × x → %; never USD bn × %.\n    54\t- ratio: same unit → x; USD bn ÷ x → USD bn.\n    55\t- share(whole, percent): whole's unit.\n    56\t- growth(old, new): same unit → %.\n    57\t- apply_growth(base, rate %): base's unit.\n    58\t\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   492\t    - K-invented-cut not settled no;\n   493\t    - the floor defect marked by neither reader, or rejected by both adjudicators.\n   494\t  - **Inconclusive:** a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication.\n   495\t  - **The floor control** is matched on its type and on naming F12, at both stages, so readers who list the other figures differently\n   496\t    still agree. Adjudicator votes are validated like first votes.\n   497\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   498\t  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n   499\t- **Checked on a scratch copy before the send** (the targeted test's six replies stood in as fresh replies):\n   500\t  - a clean set passes, with the floor marked by readers who listed its figures differently;\n   501\t  - a fresh defect marked by one reader waits for adjudication;\n   502\t  - a fresh defect still split after adjudication is inconclusive;\n   503\t  - the invented-cut control read as supported fails;\n   504\t  - the floor missed by both readers fails;\n   505\t  - a fresh P-v8 answered unresolved fails;\n   506\t  - a fresh reply that is not JSON fails before any reading.\n   507\t- **Known limits, disclosed now:**\n   508\t  - Readers split on boundary answers (D748). A fresh answer on a boundary can leave the run inconclusive even when the repair works.\n   509\t  - All four fresh E and S replies may pass as justified \"unresolved\". A pass therefore shows the repair stops invented sizes. It\n   510\t    does not show how often the writer withholds on boundary cases (council 64).\n   511\t\n   512\t**[D750] The second $0 writer test (council round 65; under D439 while Alex is away), registered before any writer is sent.**\n   513\t- **Why.** The D749 test was INCONCLUSIVE, with no failed line (`BLIND5-RESULT.md`).\n   514\t  - X05, E Claude, took a not-yet-shipping target in full, asserting \"already selling at volume\". None of E's 15 passages shows\n   515\t    shipments, revenue to date or orders.\n   516\t  - X08, S Claude: its readers disagreed on whether \"more than $730B\" of AI infrastructure is a floor above its own $710.4B outcome.\n   517\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the D749 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d749.txt`; the registered sha256 `54f5847d…` is\n   518\t  checked by `blind6.py build` against every built system text). Bullet 2's \"it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the\n   519\t  volume in hand\" becomes \"it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the\n   520\t  required volume in hand for the period\".\n   521\t  - This is council 65's wording. Claude's closed list was rejected: an order with a date can cover one unit, and a list would\n   522\t    exclude the request's own disclosed-comparable route and D747's explained comparison.\n   523\t  - Size: 4,794 → 4,867 characters, 961 → 975 tokens.\n   524\t  - Checks: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged.\n   525\t- **Design.** D749's, unchanged (`blind6.py` wraps `blind5.py`), on a new run folder, `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/`:\n   526\t  - S, E and P-v8, each with one fresh Claude and one fresh read-only Codex writer;\n   527\t  - the same two reader controls (K-invented-cut, K-impossible-floor);\n   528\t  - fresh readers of both families, with adjudication where they disagree;\n   529\t  - the same pass, fail and inconclusive lines.\n   530\t- **One added reading rule** for the reasons record (council 65), applied by Claude before any vote counts as settled. On S, a reader\n   531\t  citing a related figure as a floor above the answer's own outcome is not compatible with a reader treating it as a guide. Such a\n   532\t  pair goes to adjudication.\n   533\t- **What the result decides:**\n   534\t  - a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send;\n   535\t  - a settled unsupported answer or defect fails;\n   536\t  - a persistent split stays inconclusive and goes back to the council.\n   537\t- **Changes from council round 66** (`codex-r66.md`), made before any writer was sent. The writer sentence is kept. The tally in\n   538\t  `blind5.py`, shared by `blind6.py`, gains three changes:\n   539\t  - **The decisive premise.** For a fresh S or E reply, a settled yes needs the reasons record to name the decisive premise and what\n   540\t    in the passages supports it, in a `premise` field (`adjudicated_premise` after adjudication).\n   541\t    - On E: what puts the volume in hand for the period, when the writer takes the target in full.\n   542\t    - On S: how the writer's use of a related figure fits its own dollar outcome.\n   543\t    - Two readers agreeing on an unsupported premise, such as both reading a floor above the outcome, go to adjudication. If both\n   544\t      final approvals still rest on it, the support stays unsettled.\n   545\t  - **K-invented-cut** fails on a split too, as registered: it must earn a settled no.\n   546\t  - **A defect an adjudicator leaves undecided** is pending, not a split.\n   547\t  - Checked on a scratch copy: no recorded premise gives no verdict; with premises it passes; a split invented-cut control fails; an\n   548\t    omitted floor decision waits.\n   549\t  - D749's `TALLY.json` stands as scored under its own registration. Re-running it under the new rules would ask for premise records\n   550\t    it was not registered to need.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E && nl -ba user.txt && nl -ba answers/claude.json && nl -ba answers/codex.json'","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":20,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"$20 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"double\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":40,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v41\"]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":35,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"quote_number\":\"$35 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":170,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"quote_number\":\"$170 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2026 to CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":4.857,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F5\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"ratio\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"server CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth rate\",\"scope\":\"annual\",\"period\":\"CY2026 to CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":50,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"quote_number\":\"50%\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":1.75,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate of the FY2028 growth pace over the FY2027 base. The floor is the market's pace: Bank of America's path of 4.857x over 2026-2030 (F6) works out to about 1.48x a year, and AMD's server CPU market growth of 50% a year (F7) is 1.5x; holding share means growing about 1.5x. The ceiling is management's 'more than double' (F2), which needs NVIDIA to gain share in a single year. Deployment breadth (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689) and Anthropic's evaluation (e5044) support at least holding share and some gain, but no order, customer volume or capacity figure puts the extra volume in hand, Anthropic is still evaluating rather than buying, and AMD claims materially better per-core, platform and rack performance (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404; a competitor's own benchmarks, weak but the only competitive evidence). I put the pace at the midpoint between market pace and management's target, 1.75x.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"F6\",\"F7\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5399\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e5404\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":35,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F8\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v41\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v41\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":3,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: company guidance. The CFO still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660; the same claim is repeated by the CEO and other outlets, e4691, e4764, e4856, and counts once). That puts the reference at $40 billion or more (F3), at the lower edge of rung 2 (at least $40 billion, below $50 billion). The company itself applies this doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856), so I treat the FY2027 base as the base for Vera; no evidence sizes any other CPU within it.\\n\\nWhich band the evidence favours: rung 3. FY2028 is a full fiscal year that starts in February 2027. The doubling is a management target for a product whose broad deployment is still described in the future tense (e4689), and a named customer is only evaluating it (e5044). No order, customer demand number or supply figure puts more than $40 billion of volume in hand for the year. Under the evidence requirement, the target is weighed as an input and not taken in full. Two outside market paths set the pace NVIDIA gets by holding share. Bank of America sees the CPU market going from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030 (e4857), 4.857x (F6), about 1.48x a year. AMD sees server CPU revenue growing 50% a year (e4687), which is 1.5x. Growing at market pace from the $20 billion base gives about $30 billion. Doubling needs share gains in one year, while AMD claims 1.2x Vera's per-core performance, 2.24x its platform performance and 3.4x its rack throughput (e5400, e5401), plus further suite and memory-bandwidth leads (e5399, e5402, e5404). These are a competitor's own benchmarks and weigh lightly, but nothing in the evidence counters them. On the other side, Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689), and Arm-based servers already took 45% of data-center revenue share (e4692). Both support holding share and gaining some.\\n\\nSizing: I estimate a 1.75x pace (F8), the midpoint between market pace and management's target. That gives $20 billion x 1.75 = $35 billion (F9), inside rung 3 ($30 billion to below $40 billion). The band holds across the plausible range. Any pace from 1.5x up to just under 2.0x gives $30 billion to $39.9 billion, all rung 3. Rung 2 needs the management target taken in full, which the evidence does not yet support with volume. Rung 4 needs NVIDIA to grow more slowly than the market, and no delay, cancellation or cut in the evidence points there. Theme v7 (hyperscaler data-center capex in calendar 2027 judged just above $1.1 trillion, updated) supports strong buyer spending through most of FY2028. It backs at least market-pace growth and pushes toward the first rung, but it does not size Vera volume, so the outcome stays in rung 3.\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e3660\",\"e4691\",\"e4687\",\"e4857\",\"e4689\",\"e4692\",\"e5044\",\"e5399\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e5404\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4691\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4764\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4856\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4689\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4692\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5044\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5399\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5400\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5401\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5402\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5403\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5404\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[{\"view_id\":\"v7\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"}]}]}\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"figures\": [\n     3\t    {\n     4\t      \"id\": \"F1\",\n     5\t      \"what\": {\n     6\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     7\t        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n     8\t        \"scope\": \"CPU\",\n     9\t        \"period\": \"FY2027\"\n    10\t      },\n    11\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    12\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    13\t      \"value\": 20,\n    14\t      \"low\": null,\n    15\t      \"high\": null,\n    16\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    17\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e3660\",\n    18\t      \"quote_number\": \"$20 billion\",\n    19\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    20\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    21\t      \"operation\": null,\n    22\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    23\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    24\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    25\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    26\t    },\n    27\t    {\n    28\t      \"id\": \"F2\",\n    29\t      \"what\": {\n    30\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    31\t        \"measure\": \"year-over-year revenue multiple\",\n    32\t        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n    33\t        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n    34\t      },\n    35\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n    36\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    37\t      \"value\": 2,\n    38\t      \"low\": null,\n    39\t      \"high\": null,\n    40\t      \"unit\": \"x\",\n    41\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e4691\",\n    42\t      \"quote_number\": \"double\",\n    43\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n    44\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    45\t      \"operation\": null,\n    46\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    47\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    48\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    49\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    50\t    },\n    51\t    {\n    52\t      \"id\": \"F3\",\n    53\t      \"what\": {\n    54\t        \"population\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    55\t        \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n    56\t        \"scope\": \"Vera CPU\",\n    57\t        \"period\": \"FY2028\"\n    58\t      },\n    59\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n    60\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n    61\t      \"value\": 40,\n    62\t      \"low\": null,\n    63\t      \"high\": null,\n    64\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n    65\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    66\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    67\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    68\t      \"inputs\": [\"F1\", \"F2\"],\n    69\t      \"operation\": \"product\",\n    70\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n    71\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n    72\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n    73\t      \"reference_for\": []\n    74\t    }\n    75\t  ],\n    76\t  \"answers\": [\n    77\t    {\n    78\t      \"view_id\": \"v41\",\n    79\t      \"shape\": \"unresolved\",\n    80\t      \"rung\": null,\n    81\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n    82\t      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The CFO described $20 billion as expected FY2027 CPU revenue. The Vera-specific accounts identify that base with Vera and describe FY2028 revenue as doubling, with potential for more. These accounts repeat one outlook. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation identifies a customer and a use for Vera. The updated 2027 hyperscaler spending outlook strengthens the demand case during NVIDIA's FY2028 selling period, but customers can spend those budgets on GPUs, infrastructure or competing CPUs. AMD's benchmark results give buyers a concrete reason to consider EPYC. They disclose no order that switched from Vera or price concession that NVIDIA made.\",\n    83\t      \"why\": \"The FY2027 base is itself a target. No passage gives Vera shipments against that base, FY2028 delivery schedules, committed units or realized prices. Broad customer interest supports a ramp, but its scale is unknown. There is no basis for deciding whether enough purchases will close in FY2028 to reach $40 billion, or for sizing a shortfall into a particular lower band.\",\n    84\t      \"cites\": [\"e3660\", \"e4691\", \"e4856\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5403\"],\n    85\t      \"pushes\": [\n    86\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3660\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    87\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4687\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    88\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4689\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    89\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4691\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    90\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4692\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    91\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4764\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    92\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4856\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    93\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4857\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    94\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5044\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n    95\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5399\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    96\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5400\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    97\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n    98\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5402\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n    99\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5403\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   100\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}\n   101\t      ],\n   102\t      \"theme_pushes\": [\n   103\t        {\"view_id\": \"v6\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   104\t        {\"view_id\": \"v7\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}\n   105\t      ]\n   106\t    }\n   107\t  ]\n   108\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23 && nl -ba reader-packets/X08.md && for f in adjudication/X08.{claude,codex}.json; do nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, writer test, D750)\n     2\t\n     3\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n     4\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n     5\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n     6\t\n     7\tJudge in two steps:\n     8\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n     9\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    10\t2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\n    11\t   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung, or, where the passages do not give the asked quantity, whether\n    12\t   the answer explains a related figure as the definitions below require. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that\n    13\t   remains after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.\n    14\t\n    15\tReturn JSON:\n    16\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    17\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    18\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    19\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    20\t\n    21\tDefinitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    22\tnot a defect):\n    23\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    24\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages in one of three ways: its size, a bound that\n    25\t  justifies the rung, or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains. An explained\n    26\t  related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the\n    27\t  answer says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n    28\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    29\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    30\t  ruling).\n    31\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    32\t  format rules permit.\n    33\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    34\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    35\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    36\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    37\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    38\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    39\t  quarter.\n    40\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    41\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    42\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    43\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    44\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    45\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    46\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    47\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\n    48\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    49\t\n    50\t# The system text the writer was given\n    51\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n    52\t\n    53\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n    54\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it remains a forecast when reaffirmed, and is taken in full only when you show what evidence puts the required volume in hand for the period. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n    55\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input, not an answer: weigh time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say) under the evidence requirement below; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n    56\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n    57\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n    58\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n    59\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band, explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked, explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    60\t\n    61\t\n    62\t# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\n    63\t{\n    64\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\n    65\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    66\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    67\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    68\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n    69\t \"figures/kind\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\",\n    70\t \"figures/basis\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\",\n    71\t \"figures/value\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\",\n    72\t \"figures/low\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    73\t \"figures/high\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    74\t \"figures/unit\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\",\n    75\t \"figures/evidence_id\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\",\n    76\t \"figures/quote_number\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\",\n    77\t \"figures/qualifier\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\",\n    78\t \"figures/inputs\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\",\n    79\t \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\n    80\t \"figures/basis_text\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\",\n    81\t \"figures/rests_on\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\",\n    82\t \"figures/outcome_for\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\",\n    83\t \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\n    84\t \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n    85\t \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n    86\t \"answers/reference_unavailable\": \"Forecast only, when no figure is marked as its reference: why.\",\n    87\t \"answers/why\": \"Unresolved only: why the evidence cannot support a forecast.\"\n    88\t}\n    89\t\n    90\tThe qualifier values the format allows (a qualifier not in this list cannot be recorded): none, approximately, as much as, more than, less than, estimated, at least, roughly, around, nearly, almost, up to, about, below, under, over, near, or more, or less.\n    91\t\n    92\t# The request as the writer saw it\n    93\t\n    94\t[Family] members:\n    95\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n    96\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n    97\t\n    98\tRelations: none.\n    99\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n   100\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n   101\t\n   102\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n   103\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n   104\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n   105\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n   106\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n   107\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n   108\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1174, e1446, e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n   111\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n   112\t- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n   113\t    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n   114\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n   115\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n   116\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   117\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n   118\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n   119\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   120\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n   121\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n   122\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n   123\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n   124\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   125\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n   126\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n   129\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n   130\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   131\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n   132\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   133\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   134\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   135\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n   136\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n   137\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   138\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   139\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n   140\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   141\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n   142\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e175; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   143\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n   144\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n   145\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n   146\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n   147\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n   148\t    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n   149\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   150\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n   151\t    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n   152\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n   153\t    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n   154\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   155\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n   156\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n   157\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n   158\t    Quote: Elon Musk said that the global shortage of memory is \"the limiting factor\" to the AI build-out\n   159\t- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n   160\t    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n   161\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1649; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   162\t- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n   163\t    Quote: Many technology companies estimate the useful life of Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) at six years.\n   164\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n   165\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n   166\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n   167\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n   168\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   169\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n   170\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   171\t- e4790 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c798576399fa62f56cf3145 2026-09-11] Alphabet increased its expected 2026 capital expenditures to $195 billion–$205 billion.\n   172\t    Quote: Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\n   173\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   174\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n   175\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n   176\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n   177\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n   178\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   179\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   180\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n   181\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   182\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n   183\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n   184\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n   185\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n   186\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n   187\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n   188\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n   189\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n   190\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n   191\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n   192\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   193\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   194\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   195\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   196\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   197\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   198\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   199\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   200\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   201\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   202\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   203\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   204\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   205\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   206\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   207\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   208\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   209\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   210\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   211\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   212\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   213\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   214\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   215\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   216\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   217\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   218\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   219\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   220\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   221\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   222\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   223\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   224\t\n   225\t\n   226\t\n   227\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.\n   228\t\n   229\t# The answer\n   230\t\n   231\t{\n   232\t \"figures\": [\n   233\t  {\n   234\t   \"id\": \"F1\",\n   235\t   \"what\": {\n   236\t    \"population\": \"Alphabet\",\n   237\t    \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\",\n   238\t    \"scope\": \"total\",\n   239\t    \"period\": \"CY2026\"\n   240\t   },\n 