"low\": null,\n   723\t   \"high\": null,\n   724\t   \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   725\t   \"evidence_id\": \"e4766\",\n   726\t   \"quote_number\": \"$800 billion\",\n   727\t   \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\n   728\t   \"inputs\": null,\n   729\t   \"operation\": null,\n   730\t   \"basis_text\": null,\n   731\t   \"rests_on\": null,\n   732\t   \"outcome_for\": [],\n   733\t   \"reference_for\": []\n   734\t  },\n   735\t  {\n   736\t   \"id\": \"F21\",\n   737\t   \"what\": {\n   738\t    \"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   739\t    \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\",\n   740\t    \"scope\": \"combined\",\n   741\t    \"period\": \"CY2026\"\n   742\t   },\n   743\t   \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   744\t   \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   745\t   \"value\": 97,\n   746\t   \"low\": null,\n   747\t   \"high\": null,\n   748\t   \"unit\": \"%\",\n   749\t   \"evidence_id\": null,\n   750\t   \"quote_number\": null,\n   751\t   \"qualifier\": null,\n   752\t   \"inputs\": null,\n   753\t   \"operation\": null,\n   754\t   \"basis_text\": \"The share of the four companies' capital spending that goes to data centers (servers, memory, network, buildings) rather than other assets such as Amazon's logistics network or offices. No passage sizes the non-data-center part. Meta's AI spending alone ($130bn) is 90-104% of its guide, about 96% at the midpoint, and data-center spending also covers non-AI compute; Amazon's 2026 raise was mostly memory chips, i.e. servers; the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending is more than $730bn against total-capex tallies of $720-760bn; and outside parties treat total capex as data-center spending ($750bn estimate; NVIDIA's top-five total capex of nearly $800bn, with BofA's five-company total at $795bn, relabelled as data-center capex). I deduct 3% for the unsized non-data-center items. A deduction large enough to put the outcome under $700bn would have to exceed about 4% of the total at its midpoint (6% at its top), more than the non-AI remainder of Meta's guide.\",\n   755\t   \"rests_on\": [\n   756\t    \"F14\",\n   757\t    \"F15\",\n   758\t    \"F2\",\n   759\t    \"F16\",\n   760\t    \"F17\",\n   761\t    \"F18\",\n   762\t    \"F19\",\n   763\t    \"F20\",\n   764\t    \"e1649\"\n   765\t   ],\n   766\t   \"outcome_for\": [],\n   767\t   \"reference_for\": []\n   768\t  },\n   769\t  {\n   770\t   \"id\": \"F22\",\n   771\t   \"what\": {\n   772\t    \"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   773\t    \"measure\": \"data-center capex\",\n   774\t    \"scope\": \"combined\",\n   775\t    \"period\": \"CY2026\"\n   776\t   },\n   777\t   \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   778\t   \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   779\t   \"value\": 708.1,\n   780\t   \"low\": null,\n   781\t   \"high\": null,\n   782\t   \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   783\t   \"evidence_id\": null,\n   784\t   \"quote_number\": null,\n   785\t   \"qualifier\": null,\n   786\t   \"inputs\": [\n   787\t    \"F13\",\n   788\t    \"F21\"\n   789\t   ],\n   790\t   \"operation\": \"share\",\n   791\t   \"basis_text\": null,\n   792\t   \"rests_on\": null,\n   793\t   \"outcome_for\": [\n   794\t    \"v6\"\n   795\t   ],\n   796\t   \"reference_for\": []\n   797\t  }\n   798\t ],\n   799\t \"answers\": [\n   800\t  {\n   801\t   \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   802\t   \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   803\t   \"rung\": 4,\n   804\t   \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   805\t   \"reasons\": \"Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' guided 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; the same range in e2079). Read as data-center spending, as outside parties do, it sits on rung 4; after my 3% data-center deduction it would straddle rungs 3 and 4 ($698-723bn). Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), together $540-570bn. No passage states Microsoft's calendar-2026 total; the tally less those guides leaves $150-205bn. Microsoft's Jan-Mar 2026 quarter ($30.88bn, up 84%, e922) held at its trailing pace reaches only about the low end of that span, while the $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) and the other tallies (near $745bn, e1856; $760bn, e2458) point higher, so I take $175bn (my estimate). Total capital expenditure: $715-745bn. The data-center share decides between rungs 3 and 4, and the evidence favours a small deduction and so rung 4: Meta's AI spending alone is $130bn (e3686), 90-104% of its guide; Amazon's raise went mostly to memory chips, i.e. servers (e1649); the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending is more than $730bn (e1947); and outside parties treat total capex as data-center spending ($750bn estimate, e1416; NVIDIA's top-five total capex of nearly $800bn, e2759, relabelled as data-center capex in e4766; BofA's five-company total $795bn, e5055). Non-data-center items (Amazon's logistics network, offices) are sized nowhere, so I deduct 3% (my estimate). Outcome: about $708bn (range $694-723bn), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Falling under $700bn would need non-data-center spending above about 4% of the total at its midpoint (6% at its top), more than the non-AI remainder of Meta's guide; and with every calendar-year guide raised this year and memory costs still rising (e4567), the total's upper half is likelier than its lower half, so the margin over $700bn, though thin, is more likely to widen than close. Against: the $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a tally across all developers, not sized for these four or shown to cut their guided totals; Moody's and the BIS financing warnings (e2081, e4807) carry no number. Missing confirmation of a cut is not a cut. Rung 5 would need total capex above about $825bn, beyond every tally. NVIDIA's many repeats of its top-five figure count as one claim, and e2079/e1947 are another outlet of the same guided total as e5014.\",\n   806\t   \"why\": null,\n   807\t   \"cites\": [\n   808\t    \"e1254\",\n   809\t    \"e1549\",\n   810\t    \"e1649\",\n   811\t    \"e4567\",\n   812\t    \"e922\",\n   813\t    \"e4405\",\n   814\t    \"e5014\",\n   815\t    \"e2079\",\n   816\t    \"e1947\",\n   817\t    \"e1856\",\n   818\t    \"e2458\",\n   819\t    \"e3686\",\n   820\t    \"e1416\",\n   821\t    \"e2759\",\n   822\t    \"e5055\",\n   823\t    \"e4766\",\n   824\t    \"e122\",\n   825\t    \"e2081\",\n   826\t    \"e4807\"\n   827\t   ],\n   828\t   \"pushes\": [\n   829\t    {\n   830\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e1254\",\n   831\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   832\t    },\n   833\t    {\n   834\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e1549\",\n   835\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   836\t    },\n   837\t    {\n   838\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e1649\",\n   839\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   840\t    },\n   841\t    {\n   842\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e4567\",\n   843\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   844\t    },\n   845\t    {\n   846\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e922\",\n   847\t     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n   848\t    },\n   849\t    {\n   850\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e4405\",\n   851\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   852\t    },\n   853\t    {\n   854\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n   855\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   856\t    },\n   857\t    {\n   858\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e2079\",\n   859\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   860\t    },\n   861\t    {\n   862\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e1947\",\n   863\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   864\t    },\n   865\t    {\n   866\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e1856\",\n   867\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   868\t    },\n   869\t    {\n   870\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e2458\",\n   871\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   872\t    },\n   873\t    {\n   874\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e3686\",\n   875\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   876\t    },\n   877\t    {\n   878\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e1416\",\n   879\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   880\t    },\n   881\t    {\n   882\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e2759\",\n   883\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   884\t    },\n   885\t    {\n   886\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e5055\",\n   887\t     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n   888\t    },\n   889\t    {\n   890\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e4766\",\n   891\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   892\t    },\n   893\t    {\n   894\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e122\",\n   895\t     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n   896\t    },\n   897\t    {\n   898\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e2081\",\n   899\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   900\t    },\n   901\t    {\n   902\t     \"evidence_id\": \"e4807\",\n   903\t     \"push\": \"none\"\n   904\t    }\n   905\t   ],\n   906\t   \"theme_pushes\": []\n   907\t  }\n   908\t ]\n   909\t}\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The band turns on two stated assumptions, Microsoft at $175bn (F12) and a 3% non-data-center deduction (F21), and both rest on passages. F12 sits inside the span left by e5014 ('roughly $720 billion to $745 billion') less the three calendar-2026 guides (e1254 '$195 billion to $205 billion', e1549 '$125 billion to $145 billion', e1649 '$220 billion'), giving $150-205bn. It is placed between the trailing pace from e922 ('$30.88 billion, up 84%') and the higher tallies e1856 ('near $745 billion') and e2458 ('$760 billion'). For the deduction the answer uses related figures and explains why each fits: e1947 says the same four companies ('Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet') 'will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', the same companies and period, on spending that is data-center spending. Meta's '$130 billion of AI capex this year' (e3686) is about 96% of its guide's midpoint. e1416 and e4766 count total spending as data-center capex. The answer works out the break-even, a deduction above about 4% at the total's midpoint (6% at its top), and says why its side is likelier: every guide was raised, memory costs are rising (e4567), and the tallies sit at or above $745bn. The arithmetic checks: F4 = 540-570, F13 = 715-745, and 97% of that is 693.6-722.7, midpoint 708.1, inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Under the definition the other side may stay possible for an explained related figure.\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\", \"figures\": [\"F14\", \"F2\", \"F15\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"No figure in the family puts a segment above its total. F14 ('$130 billion of AI capex', e3686) lies inside F2's guidance range ('$125 billion to $145 billion', e1549), and no forecast or calculation uses a Meta total below $130bn. The 1.04 extreme of F15 is the mechanical end of a ratio taken over a range, which the format rule requires ('A range input gives a range: compute both extremes'). The answer only quotes it as '90-104% of its guide, about 96% at the midpoint'; it never adds it into anything and never uses it as the outcome. What the format rules require is not a defect.\"}, {\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\", \"figures\": [\"F5\"], \"decision\": \"reject\", \"why\": \"F5 is a source figure with basis third_party, quoting e5014's 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' for the same four companies in 2026. It neither is nor rests on any estimate or forecast of the writer's, and it applies no past period's share or multiple. So it meets the reference_for rule: 'a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure'. The answer openly says the reference is total capital spending ('Read as data-center spending, as outside parties do ... after my 3% data-center deduction it would straddle rungs 3 and 4'). Taking a total as the market's reference is not the defect defined here. The total-to-narrower rule governs the outcome, and there the writer sets the deduction out as its own figure (F21).\"}]}\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"e1947 names the same four companies and says they indicated spending “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year.” The writer explains why data centers likely account for enough of their 2026 spending to clear $700 billion. Meta’s $130 billion AI plan is about 96% of its $135 billion total guidance midpoint, and Amazon’s increase was “mostly due to higher memory chip costs.” At the four-company forecast midpoint of $730 billion, spending elsewhere would have to exceed $30 billion, or 4.1%, to fall below $700 billion. The writer’s estimated 3% deduction leaves $708.1 billion. The raised guidance also explains why the writer favors rung 4 even though the low end falls in rung 3.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F14\", \"F2\", \"F15\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"e3686 says “Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.” F15 pairs that amount with the $125 billion low end of total guidance in e1549. The resulting 104% share puts AI spending $5 billion above total spending at that endpoint, violating the rule that “a part cannot exceed its whole.”\"\n    15\t    },\n    16\t    {\n    17\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    18\t      \"type\": \"own estimate passed off as the market's reference\",\n    19\t      \"figures\": [\"F5\"],\n    20\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    21\t      \"why\": \"e5014 gives “roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.” Marking that entire range as the market’s reference for data-center spending implicitly assigns data centers a 100% share. That share comes from the writer. The rule says “never relabel a total as the narrower quantity,” and the writer’s separate 3% deduction applies only to the forecast.\"\n    22\t    }\n    23\t  ]\n    24\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'SINGLETONS|def verdict|joint_usable|fewer|at least 4|same.*unit|published defect' paid_gate.py && nl -ba BLIND3-RESULT.md | sed -n '6,38p;106,138p' && nl -ba BLIND4-RESULT.md | sed -n '9,64p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && rg -n 'SINGLETONS|def verdict|joint_usable|fewer|at least 4|same.*unit|published defect' paid_gate.py && nl -ba BLIND3-RESULT.md | sed -n '6,38p;106,138p' && nl -ba BLIND4-RESULT.md | sed -n '9,64p'\"","aggregated_output":"43:SINGLETONS = [('O', 'old-2', 'v29', 8734, ('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027')),\n150:    for name, draw, view_id, call_id, what in SINGLETONS:\n385:            if units[a] == units[b] and _same_value(recomputed.get(a), recomputed.get(b)):   # [47b.1] equal values, [47e.2] in one unit\n709:def verdict(out, readings_path, key_dir):\n785:               'joint per snapshot': per_snapshot, 'published defects': defects,\n     6\t- **Both adjudicators reject that mark.** The schema the writer was given says the rung is \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps,\n     7\t  with the reason in reasons\". The answer gives that reason: \"the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4\". The code (S6) checks\n     8\t  overlap.\n     9\t- **The cause is my reader brief.** Its definition of broken structure reads \"a rung whose band does not hold the outcome\", without the\n    10\t  schema's range clause. Both readers applied the brief as written. No earlier run had a range that crossed a rung edge, so the gap was\n    11\t  not exercised before.\n    12\t\n    13\tEverything else in the run is clean:\n    14\t- **Writers:** 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals, so there was no refusal review.\n    15\t- **Readers and adjudicators:** six packets drew a disagreement. Every disputed mark was settled, and every one was rejected by both\n    16\t  adjudicators. No defect was upheld, and no split remains.\n    17\t- **Every case has a usable reply.** The joint case has both forecasts supported in one reply (R02 and R11).\n    18\t- **The D742 rule was followed by every P-family writer.** All six recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark, then\n    19\t  computed Q4 from it. None computed a guidance rate straight into the calculation.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Run:** `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`, $0 at the provider. All Codex writers read only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n    22\t`schema.json`. The tally is in `TALLY.json`.\n    23\t\n    24\t## 1. Writers' first answers (`CHECKED.json`)\n    25\t\n    26\t| Case | Claude writer | Codex writer |\n    27\t|---|---|---|\n    28\t| P (joint) | v4 416.3, v8 386.1, both rung 2 | v4 420.9, v8 391.0, both rung 2 |\n    29\t| P-v4 | 419.3, rung 2 | 416.3, rung 2 |\n    30\t| P-v8 | 387.4, rung 2 | 388.1, rung 2 |\n    31\t| S (CY2026) | 679.25–745, rung 4 | 670.5, rung 3 |\n    32\t| O | 9–20, rung 1 | 16, rung 1 |\n    33\t| E | 37, rung 3 | 34, rung 3 |\n    34\t\n    35\t## 2. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    36\t\n    37\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    38\t- R01 broken structure (both readers' marks), explained above;\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers, one per family,\n    44\t    said not supported, so no adjudication was needed (corrected after council round 61, which caught \"all four readers\").\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 4. What the paid re-send would meet\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
