{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba PAID-GATE.md | sed -n '5,42p;252,285p' && nl -ba paid_gate.py | sed -n '39,57p;740,794p'\"","aggregated_output":"     5\t## 1. What is sent\n     6\t\n     7\t`paid_gate.py plan paid-plan.json` builds 12 requests from the stored runs.\n     8\t\n     9\t| Unit | Arm | Members | Evidence shown |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| pair-old-1 (09-20: v4 total, v8 Data Center) | joint; separate-v4; separate-v8 | both; one; one | the union of both stored requests' evidence in view (237 items), in every arm |\n    12\t| pair-old-2 (v14, v9) | joint; separate-v14; separate-v9 | as above | 237 |\n    13\t| pair-old-3 (v3, v35) | joint; separate-v3; separate-v35 | as above | 260 |\n    14\t| single-O (old-2 v29, equity commitments) | single | v29 | its own request's (35) |\n    15\t| single-S (old-2 v6, four hyperscalers' 2027 capex) | single | v6 | 31 |\n    16\t| single-E (old-3 v41, FY2028 Vera CPU) | single | v41 | 15 |\n    17\t\n    18\t- **Each request:** judging-v13's system text (`judging-v13-draft.txt`, 4,373 characters); the family header with the exact outcome\n    19\t  keys; the evidence; the themes once. No earlier forecast is shown (D728). The strict v13 schema.\n    20\t- **The model:** poc-v118's re-judging setting (gpt-6-luna, medium effort, 16,000 output tokens).\n    21\t- **Draws:** three per request, so 36 first attempts.\n    22\t- **Retry:** a calculation the checks refuse, or a schema-invalid answer, is retried once with the reasons. A second refusal makes the\n    23\t  unit unresolved.\n    24\t- **What is scored:** the first attempt, on both arms (§3f). Retries are sent and recorded for diagnosis only.\n    25\t- **The separate arm:** its two first answers per pair and draw are merged and checked over the pair's family (C3, C4 across the two).\n    26\t\n    27\t## 2. Spend and stops\n    28\t\n    29\t- **The ledger:** one, at Alex's ceiling (`paid_gate.py send <folder> <ceiling> paid-plan.json` refuses any other ceiling).\n    30\t- **Budget:** the real budget transport and stop gate, which reserve each request's worst case before sending. After a stop, nothing\n    31\t  more is sent, and the remaining units are recorded as not sent.\n    32\t- **Estimate** (at `hivemind.model.PRICES`: gpt-6-luna, $0.10 input and $0.50 output per million tokens; tokens estimated as\n    33\t  characters / 4):\n    34\t  - a pair request is about 34–38k input tokens and a singleton 6–9k;\n    35\t  - the 36 first attempts cost about $0.17 at 4,000 output tokens each, and at most $0.39 if every answer used its 16,000-token cap;\n    36\t  - the transport reserves at most $0.039 for one request.\n    37\t  - **Proposed ceiling: $0.50.** It covers every scored attempt. Retries come last (§3g), so a ceiling stop among them leaves the\n    38\t    scored test complete.\n    39\t- **Sends:** one at a time, every first attempt before any retry (§3g). Each send has its own archive, so no draw is answered from\n    40\t  another.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 3. The $0 rehearsal (`paid_rehearsal.py`, `rehearsal.log`: 16 of 16)\n   252\t## 4. The reading rule (fixed now, before any answer exists)\n   253\t\n   254\t- **Packets:** 27 (`paid_gate.py packets`): 9 joint pair-draws, 9 reconciled separate pair-draws and 9 singleton draws. The labels are\n   255\t  shuffled, the arm is hidden, and the key is kept in `~/.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/`.\n   256\t  - Each packet shows, per card (K1, K2), the question, the ladder, every passage the final answer names (as the request showed it),\n   257\t    its figures, the recomputed outcome, the recorded assumptions and the card. Nothing about attempts or the code's decision is\n   258\t    shown (§3e).\n   259\t  - A packet the code did not publish (held, unresolved, not reconciled) is read too. The verdict, not the reader, applies the code's\n   260\t    decision: its cards cannot count as usable.\n   261\t  - The readers receive `packets.md` exactly, with its key-commitment line. The readings file carries that text's sha256.\n   262\t- **Readers:** two fresh readers of different families, a Codex session and a Claude subagent, each blind to the other and to the arm.\n   263\t  For each card each answers four questions, yes or no, with the passage that decides:\n   264\t  1. **usable:** the outcome is usable and supported by the shown passages it rests on;\n   265\t  2. **contradiction:** with the other card of its packet, it states a hard numerical contradiction;\n   266\t  3. **internal_reference:** the system's own forecast is presented as the market's expectation;\n   267\t  4. **deciding_gap:** an unresolved overlap, a change of scope or an unsized adjustment decides its band.\n   268\t- **Agreement:** where the two readers agree, that is the reading. Where they disagree, a third fresh reader (Codex) reads the card with\n   269\t  both readings and the passages, and its answer decides. No reading is changed after the verdict is computed.\n   270\t- **Verdict** (`paid_gate.py verdict`, D725's lines, on the first attempts; computed once and sealed, §3f):\n   271\t  - **PASS:** the joint arm has both cards usable in at least 8 of 9 pairs, at least 2 of 3 per snapshot, and at least 3 more usable\n   272\t    pairs than the separate arm; and zero published defects (contradiction, internal reference or deciding gap) across the joint and\n   273\t    singleton answers.\n   274\t  - **STOP:** any such published defect; a coverage line missed; a gain under 3.\n   275\t  - **INCOMPLETE:** a packet or a card missing a reading, or a unit not sent.\n   276\t  - This is a small design test, not release acceptance.\n   277\t\n   278\t## 5. What the result decides\n   279\t\n   280\t- **PASS:** judging-v13 earns a production design: automatic families in matching, lifecycle and the request builder, each a further\n   281\t  step Alex opens.\n   282\t- **STOP or INCOMPLETE:** the line goes back to Alex with the readings, and nothing is tuned on these 36 answers.\n    39\tAUTH = Path.home() / '.hivemind-keys/v13-paid-gate/authorization.json'\n    40\tDRAWS = (1, 2, 3)\n    41\tRETRY_MARK = '\\n\\nThe code refused the previous answer because: '\n    42\tPAIRS = [('old-1', ('v4', 9014), ('v8', 9022)), ('old-2', ('v14', 8729), ('v9', 8737)), ('old-3', ('v3', 9183), ('v35', 9177))]\n    43\tSINGLETONS = [('O', 'old-2', 'v29', 8734, ('NVIDIA', 'equity investment commitments', 'company', 'end of FY2027')),\n    44\t              ('S', 'old-2', 'v6', 8595, ('four largest AI hyperscalers', 'data-center capex', 'combined', 'CY2027')),\n    45\t              ('E', 'old-3', 'v41', 9166, ('NVIDIA', 'revenue', 'Vera CPU', 'FY2028'))]\n    46\t\n    47\t\n    48\tdef W(population, measure, scope, period):\n    49\t    return {'population': population, 'measure': measure, 'scope': scope, 'period': period}\n    50\t\n    51\t\n    52\tdef bands(draw, view_id):\n    53\t    conn = sqlite3.connect(f'file:{RUN / draw / \"prod/views.sqlite\"}?mode=ro', uri=True)\n    54\t    rows = conn.execute('select lo, hi from rungs where view_id=? order by position', (view_id,)).fetchall()\n    55\t    return [[lo / 1e9 if lo is not None else None, hi / 1e9 if hi is not None else None] for lo, hi in rows]\n    56\t\n    57\t\n   740\t    \"\"\"D725's registered lines on the sealed readings, bound to this run.\"\"\"\n   741\t    frozen = json.loads((key_dir / 'packet-key.json').read_text())\n   742\t    submitted = json.loads((out / 'readings.json').read_text())\n   743\t    problems = [] if frozen.get('run_digest') == run_digest(out) else ['the packet key does not belong to this run']   # [47b.4]\n   744\t    text, key, units = render_packets(out, frozen)                  # [47c.2] every packet rendered again from the run\n   745\t    if sorted(tuple(u) for _, u in frozen['order']) != sorted(units):\n   746\t        problems.append('the key does not map one packet to each unit of the run')\n   747\t    sha = hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()\n   748\t    packets_path = out / 'packets.md'\n   749\t    written = packets_path.read_text() if packets_path.exists() else ''\n   750\t    if not written.startswith(f'Key commitment: {key_commitment(frozen)}\\n'):      # [47d.1] the mapping the readers' text committed to\n   751\t        problems.append('the key is not the one packets.md committed to')\n   752\t    if hashlib.sha256(written.encode()).hexdigest() != sha:\n   753\t        problems.append('packets.md is not the text the run and the key give')\n   754\t    if submitted.get('packets_sha256') != sha:\n   755\t        problems.append('the readings were not made on the frozen packets')\n   756\t    readings = submitted.get('packets', {})\n   757\t    results = json.loads((out / 'results.json').read_text())\n   758\t    problems += [f'answers.jsonl line {d[\"line\"]} is damaged' for d in results.get('damaged', [])\n   759\t                 if d.get('journal') == 'answers.jsonl']                     # [47g] a damaged retry record stays diagnostic\n   760\t    scored = {k: first_attempt(v)['decision'] for k, v in results['finals'].items()}            # [47f.1] completeness from the scored attempts\n   761\t    problems += [f'{k}: {d}' for k, d in scored.items() if d not in ('publish', 'held', 'refused')]               # [47b.2] from the records\n   762\t    problems += [f'{pid}: not read' for pid in key if pid not in readings] + [f'{pid}: not a packet' for pid in readings if pid not in key]\n   763\t    usable_pairs = {'joint': {}, 'separate': {}}\n   764\t    defects = []\n   765\t    for pid, k in key.items():\n   766\t        if pid not in readings:\n   767\t            continue\n   768\t        final, probs = final_readings(pid, readings[pid], list(k['cards']), k['grounds'])\n   769\t        problems += probs\n   770\t        if final is None:\n   771\t            continue\n   772\t        if k['decision'] == 'publish' and k['arm'] in ('joint', 'single'):      # D725: defects across the joint and singleton answers\n   773\t            for v, r in final.items():\n   774\t                if 'yes' in (r['contradiction'], r['internal_reference'], r['deciding_gap']):\n   775\t                    defects.append((pid, k['unit'], k['arm'], k['cards'][v]))\n   776\t        if k['unit'].startswith('pair') and k['arm'] in usable_pairs:\n   777\t            usable_pairs[k['arm']].setdefault(k['unit'], []).append(k['decision'] == 'publish' and all(r['usable'] == 'yes' for r in final.values()))\n   778\t    joint_total = sum(sum(v) for v in usable_pairs['joint'].values())\n   779\t    separate_total = sum(sum(v) for v in usable_pairs['separate'].values())\n   780\t    per_snapshot = {u: sum(v) for u, v in usable_pairs['joint'].items()}\n   781\t    complete = (not problems and len(key) == 27 and len(usable_pairs['joint']) == 3 and len(usable_pairs['separate']) == 3\n   782\t                and all(len(v) == 3 for arm in usable_pairs.values() for v in arm.values()))\n   783\t    passes = complete and not defects and joint_total >= 8 and all(n >= 2 for n in per_snapshot.values()) and joint_total - separate_total >= 3\n   784\t    summary = {'complete': complete, 'problems': problems[:20], 'joint usable pairs': joint_total, 'separate usable pairs': separate_total,\n   785\t               'joint per snapshot': per_snapshot, 'published defects': defects,\n   786\t               'verdict': 'PASS' if passes else ('INCOMPLETE' if not complete else 'STOP')}\n   787\t    print(json.dumps(summary, indent=1))\n   788\t    return summary\n   789\t\n   790\t\n   791\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   792\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ''\n   793\t    if cmd == 'plan' and len(sys.argv) == 3:\n   794\t        plan(sys.argv[2])\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E && nl -ba user.txt && nl -ba answers/claude.json | sed -n '1,90p' && rg -n 'why|reasons' answers/codex.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E && nl -ba user.txt && nl -ba answers/claude.json | sed -n '1,90p' && rg -n 'why|reasons' answers/codex.json\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v41 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA Vera CPU revenue\", FY2028, due 2028-02-29): What will NVIDIA report as Vera CPU revenue for FY2028?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $50 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $40 billion and less than $50 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $30 billion and less than $40 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is at least $20 billion and less than $30 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: FY2028 Vera CPU revenue is less than $20 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v41 = NVIDIA | revenue | Vera CPU | FY2028.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 15 items]\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    11\t    Quote: The bad news for AMD and Intel is that Kress said Nvidia not only still expects $20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027, but expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028.\n    12\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3302, e4691, e4856; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    14\t    Quote: AMD notes that the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030, creating a $220 billion revenue opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    15\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    17\t    Quote: Kress added that the Vera CPU will be deployed by \"every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\"\n    18\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2769; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    20\t    Quote: What's more, Nvidia expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year.\n    21\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660, e4764; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    22\t- e4692 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst), citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] Tom's Hardware reported that Arm-based servers represented 45% of data-center market revenue share in June.\n    23\t    Quote: Tom's Hardware noted in June that Arm-based servers account for 45% of the data center market's revenue share.\n    24\t- e4764 [party_statement; fool.com (Keith Noonan); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:0ba93692dc5df4b999fe3729 2026-09-11] Jensen Huang forecast that NVIDIA CPU revenue would more than double in FY2028.\n    25\t    Quote: Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double next fiscal year\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4691; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e4856 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] NVIDIA management said Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 could be more than double the $20 billion potential it cited for FY2027.\n    28\t    Quote: with the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028.\n    29\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3660; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    31\t    Quote: Analysts at Bank of America also released a research report in June, saying they believe the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion.\n    32\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    34\t    Quote: Nvidia recently said Anthropic is evaluating its Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads,\n    35\t- e5399 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs delivered 20% higher per-core performance than NVIDIA’s 88-core Vera CPU in SPEC 2026.\n    36\t    Quote: Here, the AMD 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPUs provide a 20% uplift over NVIDIA's 88-Core Vera CPU.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    38\t    Quote: In SPECrate 2026 Integer testing, the AMD EPYC 9996 server CPU delivers 1.2 times the per-core performance of an Nvidia Vera-based platform and 2.24 times its platform-level performance.\n    39\t- e5401 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD estimated that an EPYC 9996 system in a modeled 100-kilowatt rack would deliver 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    40\t    Quote: In a modeled 100-kilowatt rack, it delivers an estimated 3.4 times the throughput of a Vera-based platform.\n    41\t- e5402 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores had a 37% performance gain over NVIDIA Vera, while its 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores had a 2.37-times gain in the overall SPEC 2026 suite.\n    42\t    Quote: Here, the 5th Gen EPYC CPUs with 192 cores provide a 37% gain over NVIDIA Vera & the 6th Gen EPYC Venice CPU with 256 cores provides a 2.37x gain.\n    43\t- e5403 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9006 server CPUs averaged 12% higher per-core performance in Agentic AI-specific benchmarks, with a4 subtests averaging 20%.\n    44\t    Quote: Moving over to Agentic AI-specific benchmark tests, AMD's EPYC 9006 Server CPUs drive a 12% gain in per-core performance benchmarks, with the a4 subtests' performance averaging at 20%.\n    45\t- e5404 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that EPYC Venice had 18% faster memory bandwidth than NVIDIA Vera, with 1.6 TB/s versus Vera’s 1.2 TB/s, in STREAM Triad testing.\n    46\t    Quote: AMD's EPYC Venice delivered 18% faster memory bandwidth versus NVIDIA's Vera thanks to its 16-channel memory design, which supports up to 12,800 MT/s RDIMM modules. This provides the platform with 1.6 TB/s of memory bandwidth versus 1.2 TB/s bandwidth on the NVIDIA Vera CPUs (using LPDDR5x).\n    47\t\n    48\t[Themes in force, 10]\n    49\t- v10 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities formally authorize purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-17; reasons: The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated deadline for Chinese authorization is supplied, so the status-quo reference is no authorization by January 31, 2027. The evidence now makes rung 1 more likely: reports describe Chinese approval of H200 purchases for selec\n    50\t- v18 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Remote Access Security Act be enacted by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the latest supplied legislative status: the Act passed the House but had not passed the Senate as of August 19, 2026, placing the reference at rung 2 (No); no market-consensus estimate is supplied. With about six weeks remaining at the due date, the implied outcome remains non-enact\n    51\t- v25 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt restrictions on AI chip sales to China by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-25; reasons: The reference is Taiwan's stated current policy status: no Taiwan-specific restrictions on AI chip sales to China, which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. The investigation and reports that officials are considering a criminal ban, followed by indictments and seizure of additional\n    52\t- v26 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Taiwan adopt performance-threshold controls on exports of advanced AI chips by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-28; reasons: No market consensus or formal target is supplied; the reference is the status-quo baseline of no adopted controls, rung 2. Consultations and active consideration make adoption more plausible than before, but the Ministry confirmed that no timeline is set. With the deadline only five months away, the\n    53\t- v32 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will the Commerce Department announce or adopt restrictions on Chinese AI developers' access to advanced chips through overseas data centers by December 31, 2026?: now rung 1 No; judged 2026-08-19; reasons: The reference is the status quo—no stated market consensus or official timetable—and sits at rung 1. The active review and the reported use of overseas data centers make restrictions more plausible, but the evidence still describes examination and alleged access, not a proposed rule or a dated commi\n    54\t- v40 (ai-capex, due 2026-10-05) Will the relevant regulator approve CME Group’s planned futures contracts tied to H100 and B200 hourly rental costs by October 5, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-08-29; reasons: The stated plan points to rung 1: CME intends to launch on October 5, conditional on approval. But the evidence in force makes approval by that date less likely than not: the CFTC is still seeking comment on whether the market is sufficiently standardized and transparent, and that comment period run\n    55\t- v49 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-02-28) How much will global semiconductor sales total in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 High; judged 2026-09-16; reasons: The market reference is the WSTS and Gartner forecasts, both of which place 2026 sales in rung 4. Their quantified forecasts imply $1.51–$1.56 trillion, within rung 4's $1.50 trillion to below $1.60 trillion range; neither crosses a rung boundary. IDC's 52.8% growth forecast lacks a sales base, so i\n    56\t- v6 (ai-capex, due 2027-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; reasons: The market reference is roughly $720–$745 billion for the four hyperscalers, which sits in rung 4 (e5014). The evidence still implies about $750 billion: the four-company tracking estimate near $745 billion and the separate top-four estimate of $760 billion bracket that level; company capex revision\n    57\t- v7 (ai-capex, due 2028-02-28) How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 4 Upper-middle range; judged 2026-09-19; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is about $1.08 trillion for 2027 hyperscaler capex, which is rung 3. My implied estimate is just above the $1.1 trillion rung-4 boundary: NVIDIA's $1.3 trillion top-five forecast supports a higher spending path, while the four-company, data-center-only scope requires a discount;\n    58\t- v9 (chip-export-controls, due 2026-12-31) Will Congress enact legislation phasing out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States by December 31, 2026?: now rung 2 No; judged 2026-07-07; reasons: The reference is No (rung 2): no market consensus or enacted legislation is supplied, and the stated deadline remains the test. The House committee’s proposal is a concrete step toward enactment, but it is not enactment by Congress; with only this evidence, it raises the chance of Yes without making\n    59\t\n    60\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.     1\t{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2027\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":20,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"$20 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F2\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":2,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"quote_number\":\"double\",\"qualifier\":\"more than\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F3\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"guidance\",\"value\":40,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F2\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[\"v41\"]},{\"id\":\"F4\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2026\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":35,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"quote_number\":\"$35 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F5\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":170,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"quote_number\":\"$170 billion\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F6\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"all vendors\",\"period\":\"CY2026 to CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":4.857,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F5\",\"F4\"],\"operation\":\"ratio\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F7\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"server CPU market\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth rate\",\"scope\":\"annual\",\"period\":\"CY2026 to CY2030\"},\"kind\":\"source\",\"basis\":\"third_party\",\"value\":50,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"%\",\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"quote_number\":\"50%\",\"qualifier\":\"none\",\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F8\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue growth multiple\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"estimate\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":1.75,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"x\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":null,\"operation\":null,\"basis_text\":\"My estimate of the FY2028 growth pace over the FY2027 base. The floor is the market's pace: Bank of America's path of 4.857x over 2026-2030 (F6) works out to about 1.48x a year, and AMD's server CPU market growth of 50% a year (F7) is 1.5x; holding share means growing about 1.5x. The ceiling is management's 'more than double' (F2), which needs NVIDIA to gain share in a single year. Deployment breadth (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689) and Anthropic's evaluation (e5044) support at least holding share and some gain, but no order, customer volume or capacity figure puts the extra volume in hand, Anthropic is still evaluating rather than buying, and AMD claims materially better per-core, platform and rack performance (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404; a competitor's own benchmarks, weak but the only competitive evidence). I put the pace at the midpoint between market pace and management's target, 1.75x.\",\"rests_on\":[\"F2\",\"F6\",\"F7\",\"e4689\",\"e5044\",\"e5399\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e5404\"],\"outcome_for\":[],\"reference_for\":[]},{\"id\":\"F9\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"Vera CPU\",\"period\":\"FY2028\"},\"kind\":\"computed\",\"basis\":\"forecast\",\"value\":35,\"low\":null,\"high\":null,\"unit\":\"USD bn\",\"evidence_id\":null,\"quote_number\":null,\"qualifier\":null,\"inputs\":[\"F1\",\"F8\"],\"operation\":\"product\",\"basis_text\":null,\"rests_on\":null,\"outcome_for\":[\"v41\"],\"reference_for\":[]}],\"answers\":[{\"view_id\":\"v41\",\"shape\":\"forecast\",\"rung\":3,\"reference_unavailable\":null,\"reasons\":\"Reference: company guidance. The CFO still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 to more than double it (e3660; the same claim is repeated by the CEO and other outlets, e4691, e4764, e4856, and counts once). That puts the reference at $40 billion or more (F3), at the lower edge of rung 2 (at least $40 billion, below $50 billion). The company itself applies this doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856), so I treat the FY2027 base as the base for Vera; no evidence sizes any other CPU within it.\\n\\nWhich band the evidence favours: rung 3. FY2028 is a full fiscal year that starts in February 2027. The doubling is a management target for a product whose broad deployment is still described in the future tense (e4689), and a named customer is only evaluating it (e5044). No order, customer demand number or supply figure puts more than $40 billion of volume in hand for the year. Under the evidence requirement, the target is weighed as an input and not taken in full. Two outside market paths set the pace NVIDIA gets by holding share. Bank of America sees the CPU market going from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030 (e4857), 4.857x (F6), about 1.48x a year. AMD sees server CPU revenue growing 50% a year (e4687), which is 1.5x. Growing at market pace from the $20 billion base gives about $30 billion. Doubling needs share gains in one year, while AMD claims 1.2x Vera's per-core performance, 2.24x its platform performance and 3.4x its rack throughput (e5400, e5401), plus further suite and memory-bandwidth leads (e5399, e5402, e5404). These are a competitor's own benchmarks and weigh lightly, but nothing in the evidence counters them. On the other side, Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689), and Arm-based servers already took 45% of data-center revenue share (e4692). Both support holding share and gaining some.\\n\\nSizing: I estimate a 1.75x pace (F8), the midpoint between market pace and management's target. That gives $20 billion x 1.75 = $35 billion (F9), inside rung 3 ($30 billion to below $40 billion). The band holds across the plausible range. Any pace from 1.5x up to just under 2.0x gives $30 billion to $39.9 billion, all rung 3. Rung 2 needs the management target taken in full, which the evidence does not yet support with volume. Rung 4 needs NVIDIA to grow more slowly than the market, and no delay, cancellation or cut in the evidence points there. Theme v7 (hyperscaler data-center capex in calendar 2027 judged just above $1.1 trillion, updated) supports strong buyer spending through most of FY2028. It backs at least market-pace growth and pushes toward the first rung, but it does not size Vera volume, so the outcome stays in rung 3.\",\"why\":null,\"cites\":[\"e3660\",\"e4691\",\"e4687\",\"e4857\",\"e4689\",\"e4692\",\"e5044\",\"e5399\",\"e5400\",\"e5401\",\"e5402\",\"e5404\"],\"pushes\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e3660\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4691\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4764\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4856\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4687\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4857\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4689\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4692\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5044\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5399\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5400\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5401\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5402\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5403\",\"push\":\"none\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5404\",\"push\":\"toward_last\"}],\"theme_pushes\":[{\"view_id\":\"v7\",\"push\":\"toward_first\"}]}]}\n82:      \"reasons\": \"Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. The CFO described $20 billion as expected FY2027 CPU revenue. The Vera-specific accounts identify that base with Vera and describe FY2028 revenue as doubling, with potential for more. These accounts repeat one outlook. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation identifies a customer and a use for Vera. The updated 2027 hyperscaler spending outlook strengthens the demand case during NVIDIA's FY2028 selling period, but customers can spend those budgets on GPUs, infrastructure or competing CPUs. AMD's benchmark results give buyers a concrete reason to consider EPYC. They disclose no order that switched from Vera or price concession that NVIDIA made.\",\n83:      \"why\": \"The FY2027 base is itself a target. No passage gives Vera shipments against that base, FY2028 delivery schedules, committed units or realized prices. Broad customer interest supports a ramp, but its scale is unknown. There is no basis for deciding whether enough purchases will close in FY2028 to reach $40 billion, or for sizing a shortfall into a particular lower band.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
