{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'e1416 |e5014 |e4766 |e2759 |F5|90-104|reference_for' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md | cut -c1-900 | head -56\"","aggregated_output":"83: \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\n84: \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n110:- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n132:- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n168:- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n176:- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n255:   \"reference_for\": []\n279:   \"reference_for\": []\n303:   \"reference_for\": []\n331:   \"reference_for\": []\n334:   \"id\": \"F5\",\n355:   \"reference_for\": [\n377:    \"F5\",\n384:   \"reference_for\": []\n408:   \"reference_for\": []\n432:   \"reference_for\": []\n456:   \"reference_for\": []\n480:   \"reference_for\": []\n504:   \"reference_for\": []\n535:   \"reference_for\": []\n562:   \"reference_for\": []\n586:   \"reference_for\": []\n613:   \"reference_for\": []\n637:   \"reference_for\": []\n661:   \"reference_for\": []\n685:   \"reference_for\": []\n709:   \"reference_for\": []\n733:   \"reference_for\": []\n754:   \"basis_text\": \"The share of the four companies' capital spending that goes to data centers (servers, memory, network, buildings) rather than other assets such as Amazon's logistics network or offices. No passage sizes the non-data-center part. Meta's AI spending alone ($130bn) is 90-104% of its guide, about 96% at the midpoint, and data-center spending also covers non-AI compute; Amazon's 2026 raise was mostly memory chips, i.e. servers; the companies' indicated AI-infrastructure spending is more than $730bn against total-capex tallies of $720-760bn; and outside parties treat total capex as data-center spending ($750bn estimate; NVIDIA's top-five total capex of nearly $800bn, with BofA's five-company total at $795bn, relabelled as data-center capex). I deduct 3% for the unsized non-data-center items. A deduction large enough to put the outcome under $700bn would have to exceed abo\n767:   \"reference_for\": []\n796:   \"reference_for\": []\n805:   \"reasons\": \"Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' guided 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; the same range in e2079). Read as data-center spending, as outside parties do, it sits on rung 4; after my 3% data-center deduction it would straddle rungs 3 and 4 ($698-723bn). Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), together $540-570bn. No passage states Microsoft's calendar-2026 total; the tally less those guides leaves $150-205bn. Microsoft's Jan-Mar 2026 quarter ($30.88bn, up 84%, e922) held at its trailing pace reaches only about the low end of that span, while the $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) and the other tallies (near $745bn, e1856; $760bn, e2458) point higher, so I take $175bn (my estimate). Total capital expenditure\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'not yet shipping|not-yet-shipping|no shipments yet|volume in hand|at the reference.s rung|guidance (figure )?by itself' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -55\nrg -n 'never relabel|reference_for|both extremes' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'not yet shipping|not-yet-shipping|no shipments yet|volume in hand|at the reference.s rung|guidance (figure )?by itself' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -55\nrg -n 'never relabel|reference_for|both extremes' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/S/schema.json\"","aggregated_output":"3053:- Decided, the analyst's rule: (1) the rung is a forecast, the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. (2) The reference is what the market expects: consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline; a consensus or guidance figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and, once other evidence has arrived, is never the reason to stay. (3) The judge derives the outcome the evidence implies (reported figures and guidance give a run-rate; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it), checks it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole; a figure spanning several periods or products gives the question only its share), compares it with the rung boundaries and moves when it crosses one. (4) Weight follows tier and novelty: fact fixes what is known; party statements are the strongest forecast input; third-party views and rumours move the forecast in proportion to their specificity; a repeat, another outlet or an opinion with no new fact or calculation moves nothing. (5) The answer states `reference` and `implied_outcome` as required fields from `judging-v6` (`judging.schema_for`; code refuses an empty one; stored beside the rung in `judgements.reference` / `implied_outcome`; a ledger written before v6 gains the columns at its first write), so the D3 stance gold can read the reasoning, not only the rung. Written where the judgement is specified: PRD 06 State rules (`docs/system-design/new-facts.js` STATE_RULES, the generated block) and this record.\n11667:    - X05, E Claude, takes a not-yet-shipping target in full, asserting \"already selling at volume\". No passage shows shipments,\n11674:  - Claude's closed list of \"volume in hand\" was rejected: it was wider and narrower than the standard at once.\n11676:    puts the required volume in hand for the period\".\n235:        \"description\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\"\n277:     \"reference_for\": {\n305:     \"reference_for\"\n309:   \"description\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\"\n328:      \"description\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
