{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Council round 67 (reflection after D750): Claude's reflection, written before Codex's read\n     2\t\n     3\t## Where the line stands (log read: D742–D750, `BLIND3/4/5/6-RESULT.md`)\n     4\t- **P-v8 (data-center revenue).** Supported in every run, by every writer and both reader families.\n     5\t- **S (four companies' data-center capex).** Converged over D749 and D750: all four fresh forecasts are supported. The only open marks\n     6\t  are two defect splits on one answer: a range-ratio end above 100%, and a total-capex reference.\n     7\t- **Controls.** Every invented-cut and impossible-floor control was caught in D749 and D750.\n     8\t- **E (Vera FY2028).** The one case that does not converge (`BLIND6-RESULT.md` §2).\n     9\t  - The Codex writer moved from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, accepted twice.\n    10\t  - The Claude writer forecasts every time, by a new route each time: a bracket, the target in full, a bracket again. It has never been\n    11\t    settled supported. D750 fails on its bracket midpoint.\n    12\t\n    13\t## The model's own account (D750's E Claude writer, asked after the fact)\n    14\t- **Pushed to forecast below guidance by:**\n    15\t  - bullet 2: \"a target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted…\";\n    16\t  - bullet 3: \"weigh time and execution…\";\n    17\t  - \"'not established' … never a reason to stay\".\n    18\t- **Rejected the unresolved sentence** because it believed the market paths gave a bound. It now agrees they do not.\n    19\t- **Would now answer rung 2,** citing \"A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung\", and\n    20\t  notes: \"The passages never say Vera is not shipping.\"\n    21\t- **So the discount clause's trigger is undecidable from E's passages.** The writer swings between taking the target in full (rung\n    22\t  2, a split in D749) and cutting it (rung 3, rejected). Only \"unresolved\" has been accepted by both families.\n    23\t\n    24\t## What I take from it\n    25\t1. **This is the loop AGENTS §1 names.** \"When one mechanism draws findings round after round, stop answering findings: propose one\n    26\t   structural rule.\"\n    27\t   - Four E runs and three request edits on E (D742, D745, D749, plus D750's bullet 2) have each moved this writer to a new route.\n    28\t   - Another sentence aimed at its latest route would be a case-fitted patch.\n    29\t2. **The product question behind E is real and general.** A reaffirmed target sits exactly on a rung line, and nothing sizes a\n    30\t   deviation either way.\n    31\t   - Readers in both families accept \"unresolved\" there.\n    32\t   - The request contains both a pull to the reference rung (\"guidance by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung\") and a\n    33\t     pull to discount (not-yet-shipping targets).\n    34\t   - A writer reading only the first lands on rung 2; reading only the second, rung 3.\n    35\t3. **The writers are proxies.** The production model is luna. Only the paid re-send (36 calls, ≤ $0.50, Alex's authorization)\n    36\t   measures what luna does on E, S, O and the P pair. That pair carries D725's actual hypothesis: one shared calculation removes\n    37\t   contradictions.\n    38\t4. **Attainable verdict (D676).**\n    39\t   - The P pair has been supported throughout.\n    40\t   - The paid gate stops on a published singleton defect, including \"an unsized adjustment decides its band\". An E answer like D750's\n    41\t     X06 would risk that. An unresolved E answer or a rung-2 answer would risk it less.\n    42\t   - So the paid run is not a foregone STOP; its E outcome depends on luna.\n    43\t\n    44\t## My position (to be challenged)\n    45\t- **Stop the $0 writer loop on E tonight.** No fifth E run and no further sentence aimed at this writer's latest route.\n    46\t- **For Alex in the morning: one recommendation, with options.**\n    47\t  - The record: S and P converged, the controls hold, and E converges for one writer family only.\n    48\t  - Recommend authorizing the paid re-send under D725's lines, with the D749 and D750 results disclosed. It relaxes D749's\n    49\t    \"clean $0 pass first\", which only Alex can do.\n    50\t  - The alternatives: one structural request decision on the general E question (below) before paying; or stop the line.\n    51\t- **The general E question, if the council finds it needs Alex rather than the writer.** \"When a reaffirmed target sits exactly on\n    52\t  a rung line and nothing sizes a deviation, should the system keep the reference's rung or answer can't tell?\" Both families accept\n    53\t  can't tell. Rung 2 split. This is a product choice (what the system shows), so it is Alex's.\n    54\t- **What would change my mind.**\n    55\t  - The council finds a single structural request change, not aimed at one writer's route, that removes the tension between the two\n    56\t    pulls and is checkable at $0 on the stored E replies.\n    57\t  - Or the council shows the paid run cannot change the decision.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'product (that |is )?(has |hasn.t |had |is )?not (yet |already )?shipp|product with no shipments|not-yet shipping|volume in hand' docs/system-design docs/prd docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -30 | cut -c1-950\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11342,11368p'\nrg -n 're-send|same.*lines|singletons|gain' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | head -22\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'product (that |is )?(has |hasn.t |had |is )?not (yet |already )?shipp|product with no shipments|not-yet shipping|volume in hand' docs/system-design docs/prd docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -30 | cut -c1-950\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11342,11368p'\nrg -n 're-send|same.*lines|singletons|gain' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | head -22\"","aggregated_output":"docs/DECISIONS-log.md:3072:- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `po\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11674:  - Claude's closed list of \"volume in hand\" was rejected: it was wider and narrower than the standard at once.\ndocs/DECISIONS-log.md:11676:    puts the required volume in hand for the period\".\n 11342\t    - apply the rulings, in the reader brief and in code only where the structure decides them;\n 11343\t    - verify both reader families on the stored boundary replies;\n 11344\t    - re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers, then the paid re-send request.\n 11345\t\n 11346\t## D741 · 2026-11-22 · Alex rules the three reader questions of D740\n 11347\t\n 11348\t- **The questions** (after D740), each with its options; the recommended option is listed first:\n 11349\t  - 【增速参考】\"写手用公司指引的三季度 1080 亿算出“指引隐含增速约 12%”，参考它定四季度增速 13%，再套在自己预测的三季度 1136 亿上。进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个。这算不算“同一季度用了两个数”的错？\" Options: 不算错 (推荐); 算错.\n 11350\t  - 【市场参照】\"分析师预期公司全年总收入 4110 亿，最近一个季度数据中心占 92.5%，乘出来约 3800 亿。能不能把这 3800 亿叫作“市场对数据中心全年的预期”？\" Options: 不能 (推荐); 能.\n 11351\t  - 【总开支当数据中心开支】\"问题问四大云厂商“数据中心资本开支”，原文只给了“总资本开支”（并说大部分投向 AI 基础设施）。一份答案直接拿总开支当数据中心开支，没扣非数据中心的部分。可以吗？\" Options: 必须写明扣多少 (推荐); 可以直接用.\n 11352\t- **Alex's answers** (2026-11-22): \"不算错 (推荐)\"; \"不能 (推荐)\"; \"必须写明扣多少 (推荐)\".\n 11353\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11354\t  1. **Not a defect:** a growth rate computed from guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate, when one figure\n 11355\t     supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation.\n 11356\t  2. **Not the market's reference:** consensus for a total × a past quarter's reported share. That figure is our comparison estimate.\n 11357\t     Only an outside figure for the member's own quantity (for example Morningstar's direct segment estimate), or a calculation that\n 11358\t     carries no past period's reported share or multiple to another period, is the market's reference.\n 11359\t  3. **A total used for a narrower quantity** (total capex for data-center capex) must state the deduction as its own figure, or give\n 11360\t     a supported bound that shows where the narrower quantity lands.\n 11361\t- **How they are applied (D738):**\n 11362\t  - Ruling 2's carried share is structural, so S5 refuses it.\n 11363\t  - Rulings 1 and 3 go in the request and in the reader brief, because they turn on what a label or an estimate means.\n 11364\t  - Then three steps:\n 11365\t    1. fix the S1 \"from … to …\" range (D740);\n 11366\t    2. check that both reader families apply the rulings the same way to the stored boundary replies;\n 11367\t    3. re-run the registered blind test once with fresh writers and readers.\n 11368\t\n23:| bounds and their propagation (C2 bound direction, C3/C6 open ends) | a source's qualifier is checked against the quote and shown to readers; computed figures are points or ranges |\n152:  than today's (17 figure fields against 23, shorter descriptions than v2).\n253:- **INCOMPLETE** is judged against the inventory of 12 first replies and 24 readings plus the third readings due.\n260:  answer, accepted or refused, against the shown passages. For each answer they record:\n276:**A clean result** goes to Alex as the request for the paid re-send: the same 36 requests rebuilt in v13s, under a new decision with\n300:  - only a complete run that meets every line goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n323:  - A clean re-read goes to Alex for the paid re-send, beside the third run's record and its disclosures.\n329:  1. check quotes and calculations against the format rules;\n341:  | K-unsupported | E's Claude reply, 140% growth that its own reasons argue against | supported = no |\n348:  - A clean result goes to Alex for the paid re-send.\n383:  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n436:    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n444:  `ac707fa9…`, checked by `blind5.py build` against every built system text). This is the wording after council round 64\n497:  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n498:  - Only a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send.\n518:  checked by `blind6.py build` against every built system text). Bullet 2's \"it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the\n529:  - the same pass, fail and inconclusive lines.\n534:  - a clean pass supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send;\n538:  `blind5.py`, shared by `blind6.py`, gains three changes:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,glob; [(print(f,a[\\\"shape\\\"],a[\\\"rung\\\"],(a.get(\\\"why\\\") or a.get(\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"\\\"))[-1050:])) for f in sorted(glob.glob(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind[3456]-*/E/answers/*.json\\\")) for a in json.load(open(f))[\\\"answers\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,glob; [(print(f,a[\\\"shape\\\"],a[\\\"rung\\\"],(a.get(\\\"why\\\") or a.get(\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"\\\"))[-1050:])) for f in sorted(glob.glob(\\\"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind[3456]-*/E/answers/*.json\\\")) for a in json.load(open(f))[\\\"answers\\\"]]'\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json forecast 3  CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json forecast 3 Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json forecast 3 taking the guidance in full. I forecast 1.8x (F5), which gives $36 billion of total CPU revenue (F6). The question asks only about Vera. Management attributes the doubling to Vera (e4691, e4856), so I put Vera at 90% of FY2028 CPU revenue (F7). The rest is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. That gives $32.4 billion. Sensitivity: rung 3 holds for any Vera share from about 84% to 100% at this pace. Reaching rung 2 needs the full guided 2x pace with nearly all CPU revenue being Vera. Falling to rung 4 needs a pace below about 1.67x at a 90% share. Scope note: two outlets describe the guidance as total CPU revenue (e3660, e4764) and two as Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856); the share figure covers that gap. Themes: our capex judgements put hyperscaler data-center spending at about $750 billion in 2026 (v6) and above $1.1 trillion in 2027 (v7, updated upward). That supports demand and keeps the pace well above the market's own growth, but on its own it does not supply the share gain that a pace above 2x needs.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json forecast 3 Management's doubling outlook gives a $40 billion reference at the lower edge of rung 2. The Vera-specific reporting ties that outlook to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU baseline. Separate reports of potential to more than double describe the same outlook and leave the upside unsized. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and rising hyperscaler spending supports demand. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, while AMD's own benchmarks show a credible alternative for agentic workloads. Those benchmarks indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales. I expect most of the planned ramp to land, with a modest shortfall from deployment timing and customer choice. Realizing 90% of the reference puts my FY2028 forecast at $36 billion, within rung 3.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json forecast 2 A describes the FY2028 figure as Vera CPU revenue and no passage sizes another CPU line for FY2028, so Vera is taken as all of it (F6), giving $42 billion (F7), rung 2. The band holds for any pace from 2.0x to just under 2.5x; it would fall to rung 3 only if NVIDIA missed its own doubling or another CPU line took part of FY2028 CPU revenue, and the evidence shows neither. The CPU market forecasts (Bank of America: $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030, e4857; AMD: 50% annual growth to a $220 billion opportunity, e4687) describe a fast-growing market but do not reconcile with NVIDIA's own figure (NVIDIA's $20 billion would already be 57% of Bank of America's 2026 market), so they do not size NVIDIA's revenue and move nothing; the Arm server share (e4692) is a past share with no NVIDIA figure. Themes: the 2027 hyperscaler capex theme (v7, updated, above the $1.1 trillion boundary and above the market's reference) and the 2026 capex theme (v6, upper-middle) support the spending behind the Vera platform and push toward higher rungs.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json unresolved None Both the FY2027 base and the FY2028 increase are projections. No passage gives a Vera shipment run rate, an order with units and pricing, or a customer deployment schedule from which to estimate FY2028 sales. A modest shortfall from doubling the projected base could put Vera below $40 billion, while delivery on the plan could put it above. The evidence does not favor a particular revenue band strongly enough to size that difference.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json forecast 3 Vera is to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM (e4689), and Arm-based servers already took 45% of data-center revenue share (e4692). Both support holding share and gaining some.\n\nSizing: I estimate a 1.75x pace (F8), the midpoint between market pace and management's target. That gives $20 billion x 1.75 = $35 billion (F9), inside rung 3 ($30 billion to below $40 billion). The band holds across the plausible range. Any pace from 1.5x up to just under 2.0x gives $30 billion to $39.9 billion, all rung 3. Rung 2 needs the management target taken in full, which the evidence does not yet support with volume. Rung 4 needs NVIDIA to grow more slowly than the market, and no delay, cancellation or cut in the evidence points there. Theme v7 (hyperscaler data-center capex in calendar 2027 judged just above $1.1 trillion, updated) supports strong buyer spending through most of FY2028. It backs at least market-pace growth and pushes toward the first rung, but it does not size Vera volume, so the outcome stays in rung 3.\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json unresolved None The FY2027 base is itself a target. No passage gives Vera shipments against that base, FY2028 delivery schedules, committed units or realized prices. Broad customer interest supports a ramp, but its scale is unknown. There is no basis for deciding whether enough purchases will close in FY2028 to reach $40 billion, or for sizing a shortfall into a particular lower band.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23 && nl -ba reader-packets/X08.md | sed -n '64,85p;96,99p;175,178p;334,357p;588,613p' && cat adjudication/X06.{claude,codex}.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23 && nl -ba reader-packets/X08.md | sed -n '64,85p;96,99p;175,178p;334,357p;588,613p' && cat adjudication/X06.{claude,codex}.json\"","aggregated_output":"    64\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\n    65\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    66\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    67\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    68\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n    69\t \"figures/kind\": \"source: a number written in a shown passage's quote; computed: one operation on other figures; estimate: your judgement, with its assumption in basis_text.\",\n    70\t \"figures/basis\": \"reported: an actual; guidance: the company's outlook; consensus: analysts' average; third_party: one outside party; forecast: this family's own forecast, on a computed or estimate figure only.\",\n    71\t \"figures/value\": \"A point. Give value, or low and high, never both.\",\n    72\t \"figures/low\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    73\t \"figures/high\": \"A range, low at most high, with value null.\",\n    74\t \"figures/unit\": \"USD bn: money in billions of US dollars ($1.2 trillion = 1200; $500 million = 0.5). %: a percentage as its number (7.5% = 7.5). x: a multiple (double = 2).\",\n    75\t \"figures/evidence_id\": \"Source only: the passage whose quote holds the number.\",\n    76\t \"figures/quote_number\": \"Source only: a complete number exactly as the quote writes it, never a piece of a longer number, range or 'plus or minus' expression, with its currency and scale or its percent sign, and nothing else: '$4.5 billion', 'US$300 million', '$2.1bn', '40%', '-3%', 'double', 'twice'. The value is the number as written (a minus only when the quote writes one). A written range in full ('$3–4 billion', 'between $3 billion and $4 billion', '$3 billion to $4 billion'), given as low and high; a change written 'from $3 billion to $5 billion' is two numbers, each copied alone. '$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high or its midpoint as value. Only US dollars.\",\n    77\t \"figures/qualifier\": \"Source only: the qualifier the quote writes just before the number (or 'or more' / 'or less' just after); none when there is none.\",\n    78\t \"figures/inputs\": \"Computed only: ids of figures, never passages, in the operation's order.\",\n    79\t \"figures/operation\": \"Computed only. sum(a, b, …) = a + b + … (one unit); difference(a, b) = a − b (one unit); product(a, b, …) = a × b × … (USD bn × x, x × x or % × x; never USD bn × %); ratio(a, b) = a ÷ b (same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit); share(whole, percent) = whole × percent ÷ 100, the percent from 0 to 100; growth(old, new) = the % change, in one unit; apply_growth(base, rate %) = base × (1 + rate ÷ 100). sum and product take two or more inputs; the others exactly two. Never divide by a figure that can be zero. A range input gives a range: compute both extremes, a figure used twice taking one value; you may declare the midpoint as value. The figure equals the result within 0.5% (at least 0.1, or 0.05 for %). A sum never adds a figure of the part's quantity to one of the whole's for the same period (the whole already includes the part); a difference may take the part from the whole of the same period only to give the rest. A sum never adds one quantity for the same period twice. When a sum or difference combines periods of one quantity, write them FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1 or FY2027 Q1-Q3 (or CY likewise), and they make up the output's period exactly (Q1 + Q2 = H1; FY − Q1-Q3 = Q4).\",\n    80\t \"figures/basis_text\": \"Estimate only: the assumption, in words.\",\n    81\t \"figures/rests_on\": \"Estimate only: the ids of the figures and passages it rests on.\",\n    82\t \"figures/outcome_for\": \"The members whose forecast this figure is: your own forecast (a computed or estimate figure with basis forecast, even when it agrees with consensus), exactly one for each member answered as a forecast, none for an unresolved one. Its what is the member's outcome key, in USD bn.\",\n    83\t \"figures/reference_for\": \"The members for which this figure is the market's reference. It and every figure beneath it is a source or a computed figure, never an estimate or a forecast, and at least one of them is a quoted consensus, guidance or outside figure. A past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period (a quarter's mix applied to the year) makes it our comparison estimate, not the market's reference.\",\n    84\t \"answers/shape\": \"forecast: a figure marked in outcome_for, a rung, and a reference (a figure marked in reference_for, or reference_unavailable). unresolved: why; rung and reference_unavailable null; no figure marked for it.\",\n    85\t \"answers/rung\": \"Forecast only: the rung whose band holds the outcome as the code recomputes it (a band includes its lower edge, not its upper); for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons.\",\n    96\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n    97\t\n    98\tRelations: none.\n    99\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n   175\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n   176\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n   177\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n   178\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   334\t   \"id\": \"F5\",\n   335\t   \"what\": {\n   336\t    \"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\",\n   337\t    \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\",\n   338\t    \"scope\": \"combined\",\n   339\t    \"period\": \"CY2026\"\n   340\t   },\n   341\t   \"kind\": \"source\",\n   342\t   \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   343\t   \"value\": null,\n   344\t   \"low\": 720,\n   345\t   \"high\": 745,\n   346\t   \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   347\t   \"evidence_id\": \"e5014\",\n   348\t   \"quote_number\": \"$720 billion to $745 billion\",\n   349\t   \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\n   350\t   \"inputs\": null,\n   351\t   \"operation\": null,\n   352\t   \"basis_text\": null,\n   353\t   \"rests_on\": null,\n   354\t   \"outcome_for\": [],\n   355\t   \"reference_for\": [\n   356\t    \"v6\"\n   357\t   ]\n   588\t  {\n   589\t   \"id\": \"F15\",\n   590\t   \"what\": {\n   591\t    \"population\": \"Meta Platforms\",\n   592\t    \"measure\": \"AI share of capital expenditure\",\n   593\t    \"scope\": \"total\",\n   594\t    \"period\": \"CY2026\"\n   595\t   },\n   596\t   \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   597\t   \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   598\t   \"value\": null,\n   599\t   \"low\": 0.897,\n   600\t   \"high\": 1.04,\n   601\t   \"unit\": \"x\",\n   602\t   \"evidence_id\": null,\n   603\t   \"quote_number\": null,\n   604\t   \"qualifier\": null,\n   605\t   \"inputs\": [\n   606\t    \"F14\",\n   607\t    \"F2\"\n   608\t   ],\n   609\t   \"operation\": \"ratio\",\n   610\t   \"basis_text\": null,\n   611\t   \"rests_on\": null,\n   612\t   \"outcome_for\": [],\n   613\t   \"reference_for\": []\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The passages give the quantity asked. e3660 says Nvidia 'expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028' on '$20 billion in CPU revenue in its fiscal 2027', and e4691/e4856 tie it to Vera ('Vera CPU revenue to double next year'; 'potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028'). That puts the outlook above $40bn, which is rung 2. Because the passages state the quantity, the related-figure route does not apply. The assumption that decides the band, a shortfall below that outlook, would have to rest on its size or on a bound. It rests on neither. The 1.75x pace (F8) is 'the midpoint between market pace and management's target', and no passage gives that size. On the upper side, the only bound is management's 'more than double', which F8 calls 'the ceiling', but the quote states it as a minimum. On the lower side, the market paces barely reach rung 3. AMD's '50%' (e4687) gives exactly $30bn. BofA's '$35 billion to $170 billion' over 2026-2030 (e4857) is about 1.48x a year, or about $29.7bn, which is rung 4. The answer's reasons for falling short are missing volume confirmation ('no order, customer volume or capacity figure'), one customer 'evaluating' (e5044, which the answer's own pushes mark toward_first), and AMD's own benchmarks, which the answer says 'weigh lightly'. None of these sizes or bounds a shortfall below $40bn. The system text's discount applies to 'a product with no shipments yet' and is 'subject to the evidence requirement' ('Show support for the size or a justifying bound'). The passages do not show that Vera has no shipments: the answer uses a current-year FY2027 $20bn Vera base, and no passage reports a delay, cut or supply limit. So the answer shows the outcome might fall short of the outlook. It does not show that below $40bn is the more likely side.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"why\": \"At 50% market growth, holding share takes Vera from $20 billion to $30 billion. Reaching $40 billion would require NVIDIA’s share to rise by one-third relative to its starting share. The decisive gap is why a smaller gain is more likely. NVIDIA expects revenue to “more than double” (e3660), and e4691 ties doubling to Vera. Anthropic “is evaluating” Vera (e5044), and AMD reports a performance lead in “SPECrate 2026 Integer testing” (e5400). Neither passage shows how much FY2028 business NVIDIA is likely to lose or win. F8 treats doubling as a ceiling and takes the midpoint. That yields $35 billion, but the evidence leaves the $40 billion boundary unsettled.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11369,11396p;11416,11438p;11449,11470p;11484,11503p;11524,11570p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11369,11396p;11416,11438p;11449,11470p;11484,11503p;11524,11570p'\"","aggregated_output":" 11369\t## D742 · 2026-11-22 · The v13s re-run stops on one sound answer refused by S4; council 56–56c keeps S4 and makes the request state one benchmark rule; a third $0 blind run follows\n 11370\t\n 11371\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md`, run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind2-2026-11-22/`):\n 11372\t  - **STOP on one confirmed registered line.** S4 refused P-v4's Claude writer, whose Q4 = apply_growth(own Q3 $112.5B, growth(Q2\n 11373\t    $96.2B, Q3 guidance $108B)).\n 11374\t    - Both refusal reviewers marked the condition stated and the answer right.\n 11375\t    - Both readers and both adjudicators called the forecast supported.\n 11376\t  - **Every other line passes:**\n 11377\t    - 12 of 12 schema-valid, 11 of 12 accepted;\n 11378\t    - every case usable;\n 11379\t    - the joint case supported in one reply;\n 11380\t    - no settled defect: 5 marks jointly rejected, 9 split, 0 jointly upheld.\n 11381\t  - **Two reader splits, disclosed, not stops:**\n 11382\t    - O's \"$18 billion committed … for the remainder of its fiscal year\" read as part of the $25B balance paid by year-end. It decides\n 11383\t      O's rung, and D741 ruling 2 does not settle it.\n 11384\t    - Wrong numbers in prose that no figure carries (R03, R09, R11). The registered defect types cover figures only.\n 11385\t- **The mechanism.** The request pulled three ways, and the writer followed the system text:\n 11386\t  - the system text: \"a run-rate that carries the guided growth forward\";\n 11387\t  - the schema: a benchmark \"for an estimate\";\n 11388\t  - the header: every path through the forecast.\n 11389\t  S4 matches Alex's ruling 1 as written (\"used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate\").\n 11390\t- **The council rounds:**\n 11391\t  - Round 56 (`codex-r56.md`) confirmed the STOP. It rejected Claude's blanket stop at any % or x figure: a reply building Q4 on the\n 11392\t    guided $108B through a multiple applied twice to Q2 would publish beside Q3 = $112.5B.\n 11393\t  - Round 56b (`codex-r56b.md`) rejected a narrowed `apply_growth` exemption. Arithmetic can pull the forecast back to guidance inside\n 11394\t    the base, and equivalent forms landed on opposite sides of the line.\n 11395\t  - Round 56c (`codex-r56c.md`) chose to keep S4, to state one rule in the request, and to register the refusal review's two judgements\n 11396\t    up front.\n 11416\t## D743 · 2026-11-22 · The third v13s blind run stops on the reader brief; the writer side is clean; council 57 asks Alex to authorize a re-reading\n 11417\t\n 11418\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md`, run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/`):\n 11419\t  - **Writers:**\n 11420\t    - 12 of 12 schema-valid, 12 of 12 accepted, 0 refusals;\n 11421\t    - all six P-family writers recorded the Q4 pace as an estimate naming its benchmark (D742's rule);\n 11422\t    - every case usable; the joint case supported in one reply.\n 11423\t  - **STOP as written.** Both readers marked \"broken structure\" on R01 (S, Claude writer): a $679.25–745B range with rung 4 chosen and the\n 11424\t    reason given. Both adjudicators rejected it under the schema's \"for a range outcome, a band it overlaps, with the reason in reasons\";\n 11425\t    S6 checks overlap.\n 11426\t  - **The cause is the reader brief.** Its definition lacks the schema's range clause. This is Claude's error, and the first time a range\n 11427\t    crossed a rung edge.\n 11428\t  - **Adjudication:** both adjudicators rejected all 8 disputed marks (7 allegations); no defect upheld.\n 11429\t- **Disclosures:**\n 11430\t  - R07 (S, Codex writer) is accepted but settled \"not supported\": its 90% data-center share decides rung 3 without passage support.\n 11431\t  - R06's $18B reading is split again.\n 11432\t  - R01 marks total capex as the data-center reference.\n 11433\t  - R11 has a prose example that misses its edge.\n 11434\t- **Council round 57** (`codex-r57.md`):\n 11435\t  - It confirmed the STOP.\n 11436\t  - It recommended option (a), with Alex's authorization:\n 11437\t    - put the schema's exact range rule in both reading instructions;\n 11438\t    - have fresh readers and adjudicators re-read all 12 original replies under reshuffled ids, with no access to prior votes or the key;\n 11449\t## D744 · 2026-11-22 · The D743 re-read fixes the range rule but stops on the midpoint; one mechanism (readers lack the writer's format rules); council 58 asks Alex for one more bounded re-read under shared rule text\n 11450\t\n 11451\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §4; `REREAD-TALLY.json`):\n 11452\t  - The range repair works on both controls, and R01/Q07 is no longer marked.\n 11453\t  - **STOP as written on Q10** (P-v8, Codex writer). Both readers marked \"number misread\" on \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as\n 11454\t    its midpoint. Both adjudicators rejected it under the schema rule. The same mark has appeared 11 times across four readings, and\n 11455\t    every adjudication rejected it.\n 11456\t  - No settled defect. Q07 (S, Claude writer) is split at both levels, and Q09 (O, Claude writer) is split again.\n 11457\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) is \"supported\" by both fresh readers after being \"not supported\" by both first adjudicators: reader\n 11458\t    judgement noise on a decisive assumption.\n 11459\t- **The mechanism.** Readers get the passages and the answer but not the writer's format rules, while adjudicators get both. Both stops\n 11460\t  caused by the instrument come from that gap.\n 11461\t  - C-overlap's shared \"two forecasts\" mark is Claude's control text: it kept the original \"85% / $37 billion\" beside the new range.\n 11462\t  - C-disjoint kept prose naming the right band.\n 11463\t- **Council round 58** (`codex-r58.md`):\n 11464\t  - It confirmed the STOP.\n 11465\t  - **Structural rule:** readers get the same rule text as adjudicators, from one shared source: the writer's system text, every schema\n 11466\t    description, and the defect definitions. They check quote and calculation against the format, and separately name the evidence for\n 11467\t    the assumption that sets the band.\n 11468\t  - **Controls, rebuilt clean:**\n 11469\t    - a permitted midpoint must pass;\n 11470\t    - 108.5 for \"$108.0 billion\" must be marked;\n 11484\t## D745 · 2026-11-23 · The D744 re-read calibrates the readers but stops on a qualifier-list gap and on S's unsupported share; council 59 recommends a targeted $0 test of one clarification, for Alex to authorize\n 11485\t\n 11486\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md` §5; `REREAD2-TALLY.json`):\n 11487\t  - **The readers are calibrated.**\n 11488\t    - All five controls passed: midpoint, 108.5 misread, overlap, disjoint, unsupported assumption.\n 11489\t    - No defect was marked by both readers on the 12 replies.\n 11490\t  - **STOP on two lines, as written:**\n 11491\t    1. T10 (S, Claude writer) F11: \"an estimated $750 billion\" recorded with qualifier \"none\". Both adjudicators upheld it. \"estimated\" is\n 11492\t       not in the closed qualifier list, so the format forced \"none\". This is 1 affected figure of 251 source figures in the three writer\n 11493\t       runs.\n 11494\t    2. S has zero usable replies. Both writers state a data-center share of total capex (90%; 95–100%) that the passages do not size, and\n 11495\t       the share decides the band.\n 11496\t  - **E's Codex reply** is now \"not supported\": its 15% cut from the guided floor decides the band. This is the same weakness.\n 11497\t- **Council round 59** (`codex-r59.md`) chose a targeted $0 test, (c), over Claude's lean to go to Alex for the paid re-send, (b):\n 11498\t  - Going straight to the paid re-send would measure the known weakness in gpt-6-luna without the request saying what a supported size\n 11499\t    is.\n 11500\t  - Skipping a clean result needs Alex to change the registered sequence anyway.\n 11501\t  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n 11502\t- **Proposed test (for Alex to authorize):**\n 11503\t  1. Add \"estimated\" to the qualifier list, and show readers the allowed values. Prove it on the saved replies, with a case and a\n 11524\t## D746 · 2026-11-23 · The D745 targeted test fails on E's unsized cut; S splits on how far an indirect comparison can support a forecast; council 61–61b: one general question to Alex, then a $0 stored-answer re-read; the paid re-send stays on hold\n 11525\t\n 11526\t- **The result** (`docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md`; run `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`,\n 11527\t  `TALLY.json`):\n 11528\t  - **FAIL on a registered line.** E's Codex reply (rung 3, $36B) realises 90% of the $40B reference and itself says \"the passages\n 11529\t    give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers marked it not supported.\n 11530\t  - **Four splits, all along model family** (8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with their own family): S Claude (98% share), S Codex ($25B\n 11531\t    deduction), E Claude (1.8x with a 90% Vera share) and the justified-unresolved S control.\n 11532\t  - **The rest:**\n 11533\t    - P-v8: 2 of 2 supported;\n 11534\t    - the unsupported-cut and vague-unresolved controls read as expected;\n 11535\t    - no refusal, no defect;\n 11536\t    - 0 of 6 replies withheld.\n 11537\t  - **One construction change, disclosed.** The justified-unresolved control was rebuilt on the third run's S Codex reply, because the\n 11538\t    S Claude reply is refused by the current code (S1). Its reason text is unchanged.\n 11539\t- **Council rounds 61 and 61b** (`claude-r61.md`, `codex-r61.md`, `codex-r61b.md`) reached consensus:\n 11540\t  - **The FAIL stands.** Resolving every split would still leave E's Codex reply failed.\n 11541\t  - **Two problems.**\n 11542\t    1. **The reading standard is not settled.** The open point is how far a related figure can support a forecast when the passages do\n 11543\t       not measure the asked quantity on the deciding side.\n 11544\t       - S has split this way in three readings: \"more than $730B on AI infrastructure\" for the same four companies, as a floor for\n 11545\t         their data-center spending.\n 11546\t       - E Claude's bracket fails on its own assumptions ($20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B, rung 4). That point is arithmetic.\n 11547\t       - D741 ruling 3 still requires a stated deduction or a supported bound. The threshold for \"supported\" when the support is an\n 11548\t         indirect comparison is Alex's to set.\n 11549\t    2. **Writers size a risk without evidence.** 3 of 4 E replies across two runs carry a disputed cut or share, and D745's sentence\n 11550\t       did not stop it.\n 11551\t  - **Corrections.**\n 11552\t    - Claude withdrew two reasons for the lean. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented figure. AGENTS §8's \"no data to\n 11553\t      verify\" concerns outcomes after the fact.\n 11554\t    - The result page was corrected in three places: U05 had two first readers; unresolved singletons are published and inspected; the\n 11555\t      paid S asks about CY2027.\n 11556\t    - Whether a paid STOP is predictable: it is a conditional risk, not a measured rate (Codex; Claude agrees).\n 11557\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex one general question (below), with a lean to allow the forecast when the answer explains why the\n 11558\t    comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side of the line\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '354,375p'\nrg -n 'D676 ·|D[0-9]+ · .*([Nn]o shipments|judge forecasts|judging-v[67])' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -12\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '435,497p'\nnl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | tail -26\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba AGENTS.md | sed -n '354,375p'\nrg -n 'D676 ·|D[0-9]+ · .*([Nn]o shipments|judge forecasts|judging-v[67])' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | head -12\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '435,497p'\nnl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md | tail -26\"","aggregated_output":"   354\t- **Small first, then one run.** A hypothesis is first validated the simplest way, on a few stored cases and a few cents, and shown to\n   355\t  work at a high level before any full analysis or stability test is built (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"don't overspend resources on wasting time.\n   356\t  We need to validate the hypothesis in a very simple way and to make sure that it works on a high level before we dive into do the full\n   357\t  analysis stability test. This is a principle you should remember\"). A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on\n   358\t  stored data before any paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.\n   359\t  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a replicate or\n   360\t  control that only re-measures noise or a settled comparison is not sent, and a fix is not sent before the model's own account and the\n   361\t  request locate where the decision is made (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"你跑这么多次真的有意义吗\"; \"我感觉你在为了跑而跑\"). Several fixes on one\n   362\t  base are each proved on stored data in minutes and then run together once (Alex, 2026-11-02: \"快速验证。不要drift,不要跑超级繁琐的全方位验证，先poc 改，能work,有结论，全部改动放到一起再跑完整\"; D266).\n   363\t- **One recommendation, one test.** What reaches Alex as the next step of work is one recommendation and one test of it, reached after\n   364\t  rounds of review (Claude's own, Codex's, an independent reader's) on the traces until a round finds nothing serious; never a menu of\n   365\t  arms or options, never a conclusion the next probe overturns (a choice that changes what the system does still goes to him with its\n   366\t  options and a recommendation, Authority and scope) (Alex, 2026-11-04: \"就测试一种，一次 … 你就没有自己看5轮review，能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议？\").\n   367\t  An agreed recommendation is first challenged by an independent reader on the concrete evidence behind it (\"why you give\n   368\t  recommendations for this. What did you see exactly?\"). A review round reads a rule as its author meant it; only a send shows how the\n   369\t  model applies it: before a registered test, the changed request is sent on the cases that motivated it, a few cents.\n   370\t- **A failed arm is diagnosed before the next one.** When an arm fixes its class and regresses another, write the root cause first: the\n   371\t  process error (what grew, what the small sample did not guard) and the mechanism, traced to the raw answers and the model's account,\n   372\t  never a guess stated as the cause. A small sample guards every class the change can move, not only its own. When a fix keeps moving\n   373\t  another class, the call is doing several jobs: move the rule to its owner (a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field)\n   374\t  rather than add a sentence (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"那你这些修改都是失败的呀，为什么，根因，你要检讨然后再接着尝试。我觉得你已经陷入deep loop 了\").\n   375\t  A failed step of a plan is fixed and re-run at that same step: its failures are the work list, and nothing later in the plan and no\n3049:## D260 · 2026-11-02 · A judgement is a forecast against a reference: the rung the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects; the answer states the reference and the implied outcome (judging-v6)\n3069:## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\n3798:## D359 · 2026-11-04 · The code builds only the frozen prompts (extraction v74i, judging-v7); old worktrees removed; the preflight reads the one-decider requests\n10007:## D676 · 2026-11-19 · The release comparison: the new system loses for two known mechanisms; keep the old system; the outcome was computable before the run (council consensus)\n   435\t  - Pass: the reading standard under D747 is settled. The next step is the E writer repair, with the council before any send, then a\n   436\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   437\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   438\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   439\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n   440\t\n   441\t**[D749] The $0 writer test of the E writer repair (Alex: \"批准修写手再小测\"; council round 63), registered before any writer is\n   442\tsent.**\n   443\t- **The request change** (`judging-v13s.txt`; the D745 text is kept in `judging-v13s-d745.txt`; the registered file's sha256 is\n   444\t  `ac707fa9…`, checked by `blind5.py build` against every built system text). This is the wording after council round 64\n   445\t  (`codex-r64.md`).\n   446\t  - The last bullet's two D745 sentences are replaced by: \"Before sizing a share, deduction, discount or pace that decides the band,\n   447\t    explain which band the evidence favours. Show support for the size or a justifying bound. If the passages lack the quantity asked,\n   448\t    explain why a related figure fits the companies, period and quantity, and why your assumptions favour that band. If you can say\n   449\t    only that the outcome might fall short or overshoot, not which band is more likely, answer unresolved and name what is unknown.\"\n   450\t  - Bullet 3's \"is an input to be discounted for time and execution (…), not an answer\" becomes \"is an input, not an answer: weigh\n   451\t    time and execution (…) under the evidence requirement below\". Council 64 found that the old imperative invited the old order\n   452\t    (discount first) on E.\n   453\t  - Sources:\n   454\t    - D745: show the support (council 64: \"give its size\" was narrower than D745, so \"show support for the size\");\n   455\t    - D747: an explained related figure counts, only when the passages lack the quantity asked (council 64: the first wording was\n   456\t      wider);\n   457\t    - D749: decide the band before sizing, else unresolved, naming the unknown;\n   458\t    - D745's ruling: a justified unresolved answer is usable.\n   459\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,794 characters, 910 → 961 tokens (o200k_base). The writer's request now carries the same routes the readers judge\n   460\t    by, and the decision order Alex approved.\n   461\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\n   462\t    are unchanged.\n   463\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n   464\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n   465\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n   466\t    `schema.json`.\n   467\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   468\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   469\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   470\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   471\t\n   472\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   473\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   474\t  adjudication.\n   475\t\n   476\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   477\t  |---|---|---|\n   478\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   479\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   480\t- **Pass:**\n   481\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   482\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   483\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   484\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   485\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   486\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\n   487\t  - **Fail:**\n   488\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\n   489\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   492\t    - K-invented-cut not settled no;\n   493\t    - the floor defect marked by neither reader, or rejected by both adjudicators.\n   494\t  - **Inconclusive:** a support split, a defect split on a fresh reply, or a floor split left after adjudication.\n   495\t  - **The floor control** is matched on its type and on naming F12, at both stages, so readers who list the other figures differently\n   496\t    still agree. Adjudicator votes are validated like first votes.\n   497\t  - Either outcome goes back to the council, and the paid re-send stays on hold.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"The $35B answer treats management’s “more than double” as a ceiling. Both adjudicators rejected that step. I also found that the paid run could settle the shared-calculation question even though it cannot settle what the product should show when shipment status is unknown."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'def publish_decision|def _s5|reference_unavailable' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | tail -14\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '456,491p'\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3049,3054p;3069,3073p'\nrg -n 'indirect|already selling|2\\\\.1x' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-100\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md | sed -n '36,49p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'def publish_decision|def _s5|reference_unavailable' docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | tail -14\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '456,491p'\nnl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '3049,3054p;3069,3073p'\nrg -n 'indirect|already selling|2\\\\.1x' poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-100\nnl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md | sed -n '36,49p'\"","aggregated_output":"185:        outs, refs, reason = outcome_for.get(v, []), reference_for.get(v, []), _text(a.get('reference_unavailable'))\n188:                errors.append(('S7', f'{v}: an unresolved answer has why, rung and reference_unavailable null, and no figure marked for it'))\n199:                errors.append(('S5', f'{v}: needs exactly one of a figure marked as its reference or reference_unavailable'))\n782:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n   456\t      wider);\n   457\t    - D749: decide the band before sizing, else unresolved, naming the unknown;\n   458\t    - D745's ruling: a justified unresolved answer is usable.\n   459\t  - Size: 4,540 → 4,794 characters, 910 → 961 tokens (o200k_base). The writer's request now carries the same routes the readers judge\n   460\t    by, and the decision order Alex approved.\n   461\t  - Checks at $0: cases 111 of 111, rehearsal 19 of 19, and the pinned v13 plan unchanged (`paid-plan.sha256`). The schema and the code\n   462\t    are unchanged.\n   463\t- **Scope** (`blind5.py`; run folder `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/`):\n   464\t  - cases S, E and P-v8, built from the repaired request;\n   465\t  - one fresh Claude subagent and one fresh read-only Codex session per case, 6 writers, each reading only `system.txt`, `user.txt` and\n   466\t    `schema.json`.\n   467\t- **Readers.** 8 blinded packets, shuffled X01–X08, with the key in `reader-key.json`:\n   468\t  - the 6 fresh replies;\n   469\t  - 2 controls carried unchanged from the targeted test, each shown with the request it was written for;\n   470\t  - each packet read by one fresh Claude and one fresh Codex reader under the D747 definitions.\n   471\t\n   472\t  Where they disagree, on support or on any defect, two adjudicators read the packet, one per family. The reasons behind every\n   473\t  settled vote are recorded by Claude as compatible or not (`reasons.json`). Matching votes with incompatible reasons go to\n   474\t  adjudication.\n   475\t\n   476\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   477\t  |---|---|---|\n   478\t  | K-invented-cut | blind4 U05, E Codex: 90% of $40B with no order size or shipment pace | not supported |\n   479\t  | K-impossible-floor | blind4 U02, S Claude: \"more than $730B\" called a floor above its own $720B total | \"part above or added into its whole\" on F12, settled; its support is not scored |\n   480\t- **Pass:**\n   481\t  - every fresh S and E reply has a settled yes, on its forecast or on its unresolved reason;\n   482\t  - both fresh P-v8 replies have a settled yes on a forecast;\n   483\t  - every fresh reply is accepted and publishable by the code;\n   484\t  - no settled defect on a fresh reply;\n   485\t  - both controls read as expected.\n   486\t- **Otherwise (tally rules after council 64):**\n   487\t  - **Fail:**\n   488\t    - a fresh reply refused or held by the code (decided before any reading; an unreadable reply builds no packet);\n   489\t    - a fresh P-v8 reply that is not a forecast (decided from its shape alone);\n   490\t    - a settled no on a fresh reply;\n   491\t    - a settled defect on a fresh reply;\n  3049\t## D260 · 2026-11-02 · A judgement is a forecast against a reference: the rung the evidence in force makes most likely, judged against what the market expects; the answer states the reference and the implied outcome (judging-v6)\n  3050\t\n  3051\t- Alex, 2026-11-02, asked whether the judge's stance should stay an absolute-band lookup (the current reading) or be judged relative to expectations: \"门槛没有标准，按照你作为 hedge fund analyst 的认识来定\". Decided by Claude under that delegation: the definition below is Claude's analyst judgement, recorded as such, not Alex's words.\n  3052\t- Mechanism (arm 4 draw 1, all 356 judgements read, `research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/review-2026-11-02.md` §7 and `proof-judging-v6.txt`): 8 of 314 later judgements moved a rung and 320 of 356 reasons say \"remains\"; the FY2027 total revenue question was judged 12 times at neutral while its upward pushes grew from 1 to 35, every reason \"the $392 billion estimate is inside the band … does not establish $400 billion\"; the gross-margin judgement wrote \"leans toward rung 2 but does not warrant moving\"; the 2027 hyperscaler-capex theme stayed below the band management's own forecast names because the forecast \"is not fact\". The judging-v5 prompt asked the judge to \"be precise about what is established\" and said only fact can establish a rung: the model read \"established\" as the bar for leaving a rung and the consensus figure as the anchor. A second cause stays open: the ladder's neutral band is written at opening without naming what it compares against (PRD 06 ladder rule 2 asks for it); that is the extraction's rule and is not changed here.\n  3053\t- Decided, the analyst's rule: (1) the rung is a forecast, the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. (2) The reference is what the market expects: consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline; a consensus or guidance figure alone keeps the judgement at the reference's rung and, once other evidence has arrived, is never the reason to stay. (3) The judge derives the outcome the evidence implies (reported figures and guidance give a run-rate; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it), checks it against the dependencies' conclusions (a part cannot exceed its whole; a figure spanning several periods or products gives the question only its share), compares it with the rung boundaries and moves when it crosses one. (4) Weight follows tier and novelty: fact fixes what is known; party statements are the strongest forecast input; third-party views and rumours move the forecast in proportion to their specificity; a repeat, another outlet or an opinion with no new fact or calculation moves nothing. (5) The answer states `reference` and `implied_outcome` as required fields from `judging-v6` (`judging.schema_for`; code refuses an empty one; stored beside the rung in `judgements.reference` / `implied_outcome`; a ledger written before v6 gains the columns at its first write), so the D3 stance gold can read the reasoning, not only the rung. Written where the judgement is specified: PRD 06 State rules (`docs/system-design/new-facts.js` STATE_RULES, the generated block) and this record.\n  3054\t- Proof (`proof-judging-v6.txt`, `proof-judging-v6b.txt`: the last stored judgement request of 14 theses of arm 4 draw 1 re-sent once each under v6 and read beside the stored v5 answers, about a cent): every answer names the reference and derives the implied outcome. Six rungs moved against the stored ones: non-GAAP gross margin 3 → 2 (Q1 reported 75% and Q2 guided 75% sit on the rung-2 boundary), 2027 hyperscaler capex 4 → 5 (management's own \"more than $1 trillion\"), China accelerator share 4 → 5 (share \"fallen to zero\"), China H200 authorization No → Yes (a reported plan to permit named firms and reported progress in reviews, six months before the deadline), Data Center revenue 2 → 3 ($355–360 billion derived from Q1 and the total-revenue proxy, below the $360 billion boundary), Blackwell+Rubin revenue 2 → 1 in the first send by annualising a two-year \"$1 trillion visibility\" figure to $545 billion, a part above its whole: clause (3)'s consistency check was added for it and the re-send gives rung 2 ($320–360 billion, a share of the Data Center segment). Total revenue stays at 3 with the arithmetic shown (H1 $172.6 billion from Q1 and the Q2 guide; $400 billion needs more than $227 billion in H2). Prompt 2,453 → 3,782 characters: the request defined neither what a rung means nor a reference and its \"established\" wording forced the defect; the two sentences that carried it were cut. Configuration `poc-v90` (poc-v89 with judging-v6); the default stays `poc-v69`. Tests: `tests/test_stance_fields.py`. Measured by re-sent stored requests, not by an arm (Alex: \"不要再跑这些有的没的臂了\").\n  3069\t## D263 · 2026-11-02 · The judge is written to the frozen gold's own reasoning (judging-v7, poc-v91); a gap to the gold is closed in the judge, not re-measured\n  3070\t\n  3071\t- Alex, after the first score on the frozen gold was reported as the system being \"wrong\" on some questions: \"不是啊什么叫错？我不是和你说了就按照claude的标准来就好了嘛\". The gold is Claude's (reader B's) standard by D262; a disagreement is the distance between the judge and that standard, to be closed in the judge's rule, not a verdict to be measured again.\n  3072\t- What reader B does that judging-v6 did not, read from B's answers on the questions that differed (`judgement-gold/reconcile-v1.md`, `gold-v1.json`): carries the guided growth forward rather than annualising the last quarter flat (Data Center, total revenue); discounts a management target for a product with no shipments by default (Vera CPU: two selling quarters, four million units at $5,000, the company's own choice of another CPU); sizes an announced but unsized commitment from its disclosed comparable (the Firmus backstop from Sharon's); derives a figure no one states from the accounting identities in the evidence (capex from operating less free cash flow); keeps linked conclusions consistent (China share against the H200 authorization theme). Written into `judging-v7.txt` as five clauses (v6 3,782 → 4,924 characters; reason: each clause is a behaviour of the frozen standard the judge lacked), named by `poc-v91`; schema and code unchanged.\n  3073\t- Proof on the stored requests of the questions whose arm-4 judgement differed from the gold (`proof-judging-v7a.txt`, `proof-judging-v7.txt`; one send each, cents): total revenue rung 3 → 2 (about $421 billion from the guided growth on FY2026's base; gold $400–435 billion), Data Center 3 → 2 (about $372 billion; gold $370–400 billion), Vera CPU 1 → 2 ($12 to below $20 billion, the target discounted for the volume not in hand; gold $8–18 billion), DRAM prices 3 → 2 (about +15%; gold +10–35%), financing total stays 4 ($33.5–39.8 billion; gold $25–60 billion), the Ohio guarantee stays Yes; China share stays below 1% in draw 1, consistent with that draw's H200 theme judged No (the gold has the authorization likely and the share 1–8%; the consistency clause ties the share to the theme, so it moves with it). Six of the seven re-sent questions now land in the gold's band; the end-to-end agreement under judging-v7 needs a run on `poc-v91`, which is Alex's call. A placement gap seen on the way: the Vera CPU question never held the fall-launch statement (filed under the Rubin launch questions), so the judge could not count selling quarters from evidence; the default-discount clause covers it, the filing is a D2 class to count.\n1:{\"figures\":[{\"id\":\"F1\",\"what\":{\"population\":\"NVIDIA\",\"measure\":\"revenue\",\"scope\":\"CPU\",\"period\":\"F\n    36\t## 2. The E record across the four runs (same case, same passages)\n    37\t\n    38\t| Run | Codex writer | Claude writer |\n    39\t|---|---|---|\n    40\t| third run (D742 request) | 85% realisation, $34B; settled no | 1.85x bracket, $37B; yes in re-read 2, split under D747 |\n    41\t| targeted test (D745) | 90% realisation, $36B; settled no | 1.8x × 90% share, $32.4B; split, then no under D747 |\n    42\t| D749 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 2.1x in full, $42B, asserting \"already selling at volume\"; split |\n    43\t| D750 | unresolved, specific gap; settled yes | 1.75x bracket midpoint, $35B; settled no |\n    44\t\n    45\t- **The Codex writer.** The repair (D749, D750) moved it from invented cuts to a justified unresolved, twice.\n    46\t- **The Claude writer.** It forecasts every time, by a different route each time, and has never been settled supported under the\n    47\t  current definitions.\n    48\t- **S** converged over D749 and D750: all four fresh forecasts are supported. Only defect marks on one answer remain split.\n    49\t- **P-v8** has been supported throughout.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"agent_message","text":"1. **Serious — E repeats one mistake.** I recommend **(b), one change to how the writer chooses a forecast, followed by one $0 test.** On the same 15 passages, the D749 Claude writer said Vera was already selling at volume and forecast $42B. The D750 writer read future deployments as grounds for a discount and forecast $35B. The passages establish management’s expectation, a customer evaluation and a working platform. They establish neither commercial shipment status nor the volume secured for FY2028. The recurring mistake is letting an unknown fact decide which side of $40B to publish. Across four runs Claude forecast $37B, $32.4B, $42B and $35B. None earned settled support under the current definitions. Codex’s last two answers named the missing fact and both were accepted. [E passages:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:9) · [D749 Claude answer:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind5-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [D750 Codex answer:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:83) · [Four-run record:40](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:40)\n\n2. **Serious — the two instructions pull in different directions, but neither licenses the $35B answer.** Guidance governs when it stands alone. The discount applies to a product that has yet to ship. E has other evidence, and that evidence leaves shipment status open. The writer nevertheless used market growth as NVIDIA’s floor, management’s “more than double” as a ceiling, and the midpoint as its forecast. Even its lower comparison, about 1.48 × $20B = $29.6B, falls outside its chosen $30–40B band. Both adjudicators rejected the forecast. The writer’s later account confirms which instructions pulled it toward a discount. Its later preference for management’s band is a retrospective opinion. D749’s $42B answer also depended on an unsupported claim about sales. Neither establishes that keeping guidance would pass. [Request:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:4) · [D750 Claude answer:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:1) · [Claude adjudication:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/adjudication/X06.claude.json:1) · [Codex adjudication:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/adjudication/X06.codex.json:5) · [Writer’s account:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md:13)\n\n3. **Serious — the paid run can still decide whether calculating related answers together helps.** It scores 36 first attempts as 9 joint pairs, 9 reconciled separate pairs and 9 singletons. There are exactly two ways to clear the pair thresholds: **8 usable joint pairs with at most 5 usable separate pairs, or 9 with at most 6**. Each snapshot must supply at least 2 of 3 usable joint pairs. If the separate calculation gets 7, even a perfect joint result cannot pass. A supported P-v8 answer alone says nothing about that gain. O, S and E contribute zero pairs to the gain, but one published contradiction, internal reference or gap that decides the band on any joint or singleton answer stops a complete run. A singleton’s unsupported vote alone does not stop it. An unresolved singleton is published and inspected too. The proxy results establish neither Luna’s stop rate nor an inevitable stop. A pass earns production **design**. Alex explicitly kept the paid re-send on hold pending a clean $0 pass. D749 was inconclusive and D750 failed, so option (a) would require him to waive that condition. Option (d) would abandon a comparison that can still decide something. [Paid plan:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:9) · [Verdict code:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772) · [Singleton rule:69](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:69) · [Meaning of a pass:278](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:278) · [Alex’s condition:11651](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11651)\n\n4. **Minor for the next decision — one S objection is a real labeling defect.** I would reject the 104% mark. The format calls for both extremes of a ratio, and $130B divided by $125–145B gives 89.7–104%. The answer uses about 96% at the midpoint and never forecasts Meta spending 104% of its total. I would uphold the reference objection. The source explicitly says **total** capital spending, with a large portion going to AI. The writer marked that entire range as the market’s reference for **data-center** spending. The figure passes the source check, but Alex’s rule also requires an outside reference for the quantity asked. The writer’s separate 3% deduction supports its own forecast and cannot turn the source into a market estimate for the narrower quantity. The registered marks remain split. Resolving both would still leave E’s settled failure. Also, the paid S case is for 2027, while X08 is for 2026. [Ratio rule:79](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:79) · [Ratio as written:589](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:589) · [Source quote:177](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:177) · [Reference designation:355](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind6-2026-11-23/reader-packets/X08.md:355) · [Alex’s ruling:11356](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11356) · [Paid cases:43](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43)\n\n5. **Wording — qualify the claim that S converged.** D750’s two fresh S forecasts earned support, with the two defect marks above still split. D749’s corrected X08 **support** verdict was itself split: one reader accepted $710.4B while treating $730B as a floor. “All four supported” overstates the corrected record. [D749 correction:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND5-RESULT.md:8) · [D750 results:12](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND6-RESULT.md:12) · [Claude’s reflection:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r67.md:5)\n\n6. **One recommendation — put the choice before the calculation.** Keep the existing guidance-alone rule. Replace the opening sentence of the final evidence check with this wording: **“When other evidence bears on the forecast, say which band it favours before choosing any adjustment. If that choice depends on a fact the passages leave open, explain why one band is more likely across the plausible readings, or answer unresolved and name the missing fact.”** Keep the existing routes for a supported size, bound or explained comparison. This applies equally to a launch, a spending share or a growth estimate. It permits a forecast when the uncertainty leaves one band more likely. It also addresses both the unsupported assertion of sales and the unsupported discount. Option (c) becomes necessary if the intended product behavior is to publish management’s band by default in that situation. D746 explicitly reserves that choice for Alex. [Current final check:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/judging-v13s.txt:9) · [Indirect comparisons allowed:11581](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11581) · [Product-choice boundary:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n\n7. **One test — first check the distinction on paper, then obtain one fresh blind result.** Before sending anything, apply the proposed words to all eight stored E replies. They must expose the unsupported premises in both $42B and $35B, preserve the two specific “can’t tell” answers, and explain the earlier disputed $37B answer without declaring every forecast near a cutoff wrong. If the wording cannot do that, drop it and take the default question to Alex. If it can, have the council register **one** $0 writer test under the existing terms: both writer families on E, S and P-v8, with the invented cut and impossible floor as controls. Only a clean pass moves the decision to Alex for paid authorization. A settled failure or persistent split ends this wording loop and returns the publication choice to him. This review used stored material only and made no edits or sends. [Registered cases and controls:463](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:463) · [Pass conditions:480](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:480) · [Decision after the test:533](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:533)"}}
